WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.535
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4483 Begin Session I.]

2
00:00:01.535 --> 00:00:02.814
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>So we can go ahead.

3
00:00:02.814 --> 00:00:03.581
Everything looks good.

4
00:00:03.581 --> 00:00:03.837
<v VERA PAYNE>Okay.

5
00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.628
<v ARSENEAULT>Today is July third, Memorial Day weekend.

6
00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:06.907
I’m with Ms. Vera Payne.

7
00:00:06.907 --> 00:00:08.186
This is her second interview.

8
00:00:08.186 --> 00:00:11.000
But you didn’t get interviewed with me, I don't think.

9
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.515
<v PAYNE>I don't think so.

10
00:00:12.515 --> 00:00:12.894
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

11
00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:18.576
This is her second interview for the oral history center in the Mossville [Louisiana] project.

12
00:00:18.576 --> 00:00:28.424
And we just want to thank you so much for taking the time to share your memories with us and to document the history of Mossville.

13
00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:37.136
And we’re in her lovely home on Moss Road and she’s getting ready to move out, so she’s starting to pack everything up.

14
00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:40.545
So thank you for talking with me, Ms. Vera.

15
00:00:40.545 --> 00:00:41.303
<v PAYNE>You’re welcome.

16
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:47.742
<v ARSENEAULT>The first question I wanted to ask you is could you define the geographical boundaries of Mossville?

17
00:00:47.742 --> 00:00:52.667
We’ve been having a hard time figuring out where it starts and stops.

18
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:53.045
<v PAYNE>Okay.

19
00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:56.076
There is an area now called Trousdale Road.

20
00:00:56.076 --> 00:01:02.515
But east of that, possibly a quarter of a mile, was the old Trousdale Road [Joy Hill].

21
00:01:02.515 --> 00:01:09.333
So Mossville really started from there all the way to where Coach Williams is in that particular area.

22
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:13.500
Bel Air came in probably in the late ‘40s or ‘50s.

23
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.803
That would be that area northeast of the school, back in there [towards Westlake].

24
00:01:18.803 --> 00:01:26.000
But the old community really started from the old Trousdale all the way to around Coach Williams Road.

25
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:26.384
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

26
00:01:26.384 --> 00:01:32.145
How has the community changed within your lifetime as far as physical appearance is concerned?

27
00:01:32.145 --> 00:01:36.754
<v PAYNE>Physical appearance is as a child I didn’t appreciate it as much.

28
00:01:36.754 --> 00:01:39.442
But it was the greenery, the trees.

29
00:01:39.442 --> 00:01:44.819
It was just an area where you could feel peace, comfortable, and whatever else.

30
00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:50.964
Growing up as a child, I think it was one of the best places on earth.

31
00:01:50.964 --> 00:01:52.884
But of course I’m biased.

32
00:01:52.884 --> 00:01:59.029
And through the years with industry it’s pushed a little farther west continuously and whatever else.

33
00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:00.181
So it’s changed.

34
00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:07.094
And the last year and a half, definitely it’s changed with the families relocating and things like that.

35
00:02:07.094 --> 00:02:10.935
So I almost live in a ghost town right now.

36
00:02:10.935 --> 00:02:13.623
<v ARSENEAULT>Probably a lot different than it was.

37
00:02:13.623 --> 00:02:14.007
<v PAYNE>Yes.

38
00:02:14.007 --> 00:02:14.775
Quite different.

39
00:02:14.775 --> 00:02:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were the community leaders when you were growing up?

40
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:28.867
<v PAYNE>When I was growing up as a child, Dallas Moss, [Frank] Jim Johnson, Josh Rigmaiden, Jacob Rigmaiden, but mainly the first three.

41
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:32.006
Dallas Moss, Jim Johnson and Josh Rigmaiden.

42
00:02:32.006 --> 00:02:35.145
They were pretty active in the area.

43
00:02:35.145 --> 00:02:39.630
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did they do to kind of—?

44
00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.424
<v PAYNE>Josh was Della’s father.

45
00:02:41.424 --> 00:02:47.255
And he was instrumental in the recreation center and in the other facilities.

46
00:02:47.255 --> 00:02:48.600
His brother, [C.B.

47
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:56.673
Rigmaiden] one of them helped donate the land for the school [Mossville High], where the school site is.

48
00:02:56.673 --> 00:03:08.333
And Della laughs that whenever someone would go to jail, her dad was unofficially the mayor, so he would call and get them out of jail.

49
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:13.267
Dallas Moss, he was very, very instrumental in that [community affairs].

50
00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:19.994
And Jim Johnson, it just so happened that when we got public water out here—

51
00:03:19.994 --> 00:03:20.891
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wow.

52
00:03:20.891 --> 00:03:22.236
A service contract.

53
00:03:22.236 --> 00:03:27.170
<v PAYNE>That’s a copy and his signature is down at the bottom.

54
00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:33.000
Everybody called him Jim, but his first name was really Frank Johnson.

55
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.492
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, so you all got water in March sixth of 1969.

56
00:03:36.492 --> 00:03:38.714
What had you all done before that?

57
00:03:38.714 --> 00:03:39.984
<v PAYNE>We had well water.

58
00:03:39.984 --> 00:03:40.302
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

59
00:03:40.302 --> 00:03:45.381
<v PAYNE>In my parents’ home over there [4019 Perkins Avenue], the old well site is still there.

60
00:03:45.381 --> 00:03:53.000
I think most of them [wells] ran somewhere between probably thirty-five and 100 foot deep, and that’s what kind of water we had.

61
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.884
<v ARSENEAULT>So everyone had their own well.

62
00:03:54.884 --> 00:03:56.454
<v PAYNE>Everyone had their own wells.

63
00:03:56.454 --> 00:03:57.082
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

64
00:03:57.082 --> 00:03:59.908
What happened if it flooded or broke or something?

65
00:03:59.908 --> 00:04:07.758
<v PAYNE>You had to repair it. [laughs] And if it flooded, the only flood that I remember ever having in this area was after [Hurricane] Audrey.

66
00:04:07.758 --> 00:04:10.271
Otherwise, water drained off fairly well and whatever.

67
00:04:10.271 --> 00:04:13.097
Don’t really remember our pump breaking that very much.

68
00:04:13.097 --> 00:04:15.923
A few, ours was the electrical and mechanical pump.

69
00:04:15.923 --> 00:04:19.691
A few others still had some hand pumps and whatever they use.

70
00:04:19.691 --> 00:04:21.889
But not that very many of them.

71
00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:25.029
Most of them had converted to a deep water well.

72
00:04:25.029 --> 00:04:29.425
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were a . . . You were in your twenties when they got water here?

73
00:04:29.425 --> 00:04:29.739
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

74
00:04:29.739 --> 00:04:30.367
Mm hmm.

75
00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:32.565
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what it was like?

76
00:04:32.565 --> 00:04:35.705
<v PAYNE>At that particular time, I was not here in 1969.

77
00:04:35.705 --> 00:04:38.845
I left to go to California for about eight years.

78
00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:41.671
So when I came back, they had city water.

79
00:04:41.671 --> 00:04:47.638
And then I guess about twelve, fifteen years ago, if that long, that’s when they put in public sewers.

80
00:04:47.638 --> 00:04:49.208
And that was a blessing.

81
00:04:49.208 --> 00:04:51.406
Most of us had mechanical sewer systems.

82
00:04:51.406 --> 00:04:53.918
Some still had septic tanks and whatever else.

83
00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:58.000
So it helped our environment tremendously by getting rid of that [waste].

84
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.811
<v ARSENEAULT>So you had to treat that yourself?

85
00:05:00.811 --> 00:05:01.213
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

86
00:05:01.213 --> 00:05:02.016
Mm hmm.

87
00:05:02.016 --> 00:05:04.827
Those who had the septic tanks now.

88
00:05:04.827 --> 00:05:13.260
And then the mechanical sewer system, every so often we’d have to have someone come out and take care of that.

89
00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.276
They would clean them out and different things like that.

90
00:05:17.276 --> 00:05:21.693
<v ARSENEAULT>What can you tell me about the street names of Mossville?

91
00:05:21.693 --> 00:05:25.307
<v PAYNE>Prater Road is named after my grandfather [Earthy Prater].

92
00:05:25.307 --> 00:05:30.126
It was nothing but woods from Old Spanish Trail to Highway 90.

93
00:05:30.126 --> 00:05:36.953
And once he moved from the corner of Evergreen and Old Spanish Trail, which was a large,

94
00:05:36.953 --> 00:05:43.378
large house that had a hall that ran from the front door to the back door.

95
00:05:43.378 --> 00:05:49.000
The Praters lived on one side and the Lemelles lived on the other side.

96
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.008
So my grandfather, Earthy Prater,

97
00:05:51.008 --> 00:06:01.449
took a horse and a wagon and kind of carved out a trail that we now call Prater Road and he built their home on that.

98
00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:06.669
And the home was still standing until Rita and Katrina [hurricanes] came through.

99
00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:09.882
And it was demolished at that particular time.

100
00:06:09.882 --> 00:06:15.906
<c.p>These particular areas in here, Moss Avenue is named after, I believe, the Edy Mosses,

101
00:06:15.906 --> 00:06:21.528
because that’s who lived across the street where the big, big oak trees are.

102
00:06:21.528 --> 00:06:32.772
And then some of the others was just named after local people or some of the merchants that had came in within the last forty or fifty years.

103
00:06:32.772 --> 00:06:39.197
<v ARSENEAULT>So if you lived here long enough, then the road just got named after you eventually?

104
00:06:39.197 --> 00:06:40.000
<v PAYNE>Right.

105
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:40.706
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s easier.

106
00:06:40.706 --> 00:06:41.765
<v PAYNE>More or less.

107
00:06:41.765 --> 00:06:46.000
I think they named Coach Williams about maybe three, four years ago.

108
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:54.471
And I think they named it because he had been in this area for a number of years and had worked as a teacher,

109
00:06:54.471 --> 00:06:58.353
a principal, and then on the sheriff department force and everything.

110
00:06:58.353 --> 00:07:01.882
So they gave it to him as an honorary name.

111
00:07:01.882 --> 00:07:02.588
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

112
00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:09.647
<v PAYNE>And Benjamin, possibly, I can’t swear to it, on the next corner over there, was a man that was blind.

113
00:07:09.647 --> 00:07:12.471
And his first name was Benny [Benjamin Harris].

114
00:07:12.471 --> 00:07:16.000
So more than likely it was named after him.

115
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.320
<v ARSENEAULT>So most of the street names are family—

116
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.150
<v PAYNE>Family names.

117
00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:20.980
Mm hmm.

118
00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:21.810
<v ARSENEAULT>--last names.

119
00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:26.374
What about the King and the Prince and the Duchess roads?

120
00:07:26.374 --> 00:07:27.619
Was that Mossville?

121
00:07:27.619 --> 00:07:32.184
<v PAYNE>That was a subdivision that was probably added in the ‘50s.

122
00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:35.503
Because when I went, I graduated in 1962.

123
00:07:35.503 --> 00:07:42.558
And in that area, there was maybe at that time ten, twelve homes that was back there.

124
00:07:42.558 --> 00:07:50.442
So someone had the property, divided it into subdivisions, and it was that person who decided to name it.

125
00:07:50.442 --> 00:07:55.837
I’m thinking it was someone who was white who had purchased the property.

126
00:07:55.837 --> 00:08:02.061
And then they made the subdivisions and named it what they wanted to name it.

127
00:08:02.061 --> 00:08:10.361
Because across the street over there [Perkins Avenue], unless it’s changed recently, Powell Lumber owned all of that over there.

128
00:08:10.361 --> 00:08:17.000
So it’s possible that they could have sold a tract of land, whatever, added that.

129
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:17.808
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

130
00:08:17.808 --> 00:08:21.038
Could you describe the weddings held in Mossville?

131
00:08:21.038 --> 00:08:26.288
You were telling me earlier how the whole community is like a family.

132
00:08:26.288 --> 00:08:27.096
<v PAYNE>It is.

133
00:08:27.096 --> 00:08:29.923
<v ARSENEAULT>So did everyone come to the wedding?

134
00:08:29.923 --> 00:08:38.000
<v PAYNE>Yes. [laughs] You didn’t really have [to send invitations, but some did], if it was family, you just showed up.

135
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:44.865
You didn’t have to worry about an invitation or feeling out of place or anything like that.

136
00:08:44.865 --> 00:08:47.692
Some of the weddings were church weddings.

137
00:08:47.692 --> 00:08:51.327
Some weddings were in the home and whatever else.

138
00:08:51.327 --> 00:08:54.558
It was just almost like a family reunion.

139
00:08:54.558 --> 00:09:01.827
And you never had to worry about someone feeling you were out of place or anything like that.

140
00:09:01.827 --> 00:09:03.038
It was beautiful.

141
00:09:03.038 --> 00:09:07.885
My sister [Margaret] and I were fifteen years younger than each other.

142
00:09:07.885 --> 00:09:12.327
And she got married roughly about twenty-five or thirty years ago.

143
00:09:12.327 --> 00:09:15.154
And the entire family was at church.

144
00:09:15.154 --> 00:09:20.000
And afterwards for the reception and everything, we were a community.

145
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:28.464
This is a little bit off of your point of view or whatever, but I was thinking about this last night.

146
00:09:28.464 --> 00:09:33.703
In the ‘60s, when integration and all started to take on a momentum,

147
00:09:33.703 --> 00:09:40.555
when I graduated from high school in ’62, I didn’t feel any of that pressure or anything.

148
00:09:40.555 --> 00:09:45.392
And I think it was because we were such a close-knit community.

149
00:09:45.392 --> 00:09:47.810
And everybody felt a part of.

150
00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:49.422
And you were accepted.

151
00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:53.856
And it just really didn’t faze me or anything like that.

152
00:09:53.856 --> 00:10:03.932
I was away when the kids had to be bused out to other schools, so I can’t attest to what they went through or whatever.

153
00:10:03.932 --> 00:10:11.992
But I never experienced that because I had that family and I had that acceptance and whatever else was here.

154
00:10:11.992 --> 00:10:22.068
And by the time I moved to California, it was already miles ahead of what Louisiana was, so I didn’t go through any of that.

155
00:10:22.068 --> 00:10:30.129
But yeah we used to have family picnics and Mom had, she’s the only surviving of four of the siblings,

156
00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:35.772
three sisters and a brother, and we all got together and had family picnics.

157
00:10:35.772 --> 00:10:42.624
And I had an Aunt Viola and my Aunt Alean who just passed about two weeks ago.

158
00:10:42.624 --> 00:10:45.042
They were the center of attraction.

159
00:10:45.042 --> 00:10:48.669
And then later my Aunt Alean’s daughter joined in.

160
00:10:48.669 --> 00:10:57.133
You never had to worry about entertainment or games or anything like that, because they made sure that there was always

161
00:10:57.133 --> 00:10:57.536
<c.p>entertainment.

162
00:10:57.536 --> 00:11:06.000
And we had a joke in our family that Aunt Viola could slice a cake thinner than anybody that we—[laughs]

163
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:07.806
<v ARSENEAULT>To make it go around?

164
00:11:07.806 --> 00:11:08.168
<v PAYNE>Yes.

165
00:11:08.168 --> 00:11:10.335
She would make it go around.

166
00:11:10.335 --> 00:11:16.839
And that possibly might have been inherited, because I had a great-grandmother and her name was Mary Vincent.

167
00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:21.174
She passed away probably when I was four or five years old.

168
00:11:21.174 --> 00:11:24.426
And I only have one visual picture of her.

169
00:11:24.426 --> 00:11:30.568
And that was standing in the kitchen facing the woodstove, her back was to me and whatever.

170
00:11:30.568 --> 00:11:39.239
And Mom would tell the tale that when they were growing up, if she was down to one egg and there were three kids,

171
00:11:39.239 --> 00:11:45.742
and she cooked that one egg, she was going to divide that one egg between those three kids.

172
00:11:45.742 --> 00:11:49.355
And that’s what kind of family I came up with.

173
00:11:49.355 --> 00:11:55.497
Mom and Dad rented from Aunt Ava and Uncle Leo [Prater] the first years of their marriage.

174
00:11:55.497 --> 00:12:01.277
And then probably in the mid-‘50s they built a home over here on the next street.

175
00:12:01.277 --> 00:12:05.252
And shortly afterwards my Aunt Alean built a home over there.

176
00:12:05.252 --> 00:12:08.142
Hers is still standing, the little green house.

177
00:12:08.142 --> 00:12:14.645
And then her sister Elnora built at the end of the street [bought a home on Moss Avenue].

178
00:12:14.645 --> 00:12:20.787
So it was like no matter where the family went, we migrated toward each other and whatever.

179
00:12:20.787 --> 00:12:22.955
So we just had a ball.

180
00:12:22.955 --> 00:12:23.316
Yeah.

181
00:12:23.316 --> 00:12:28.013
<v ARSENEAULT>So the wedding would, like would your mom cook or the mothers cook?

182
00:12:28.013 --> 00:12:32.710
<v PAYNE>Most of the time, at that particular time, my grandmother was still alive.

183
00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:35.239
So she did a lot of cooking.

184
00:12:35.239 --> 00:12:40.297
And then my Aunt Viola, who I told you about the cake, she would

185
00:12:40.297 --> 00:12:42.465
<c.p>do a lot of cooking, also.

186
00:12:42.465 --> 00:12:46.439
And then various ones would bring different dishes and whatever else.

187
00:12:46.439 --> 00:12:48.968
And that way we made it easier.

188
00:12:48.968 --> 00:12:51.135
There wasn’t a lot of money.

189
00:12:51.135 --> 00:12:56.916
So everybody pitched in and did what they had to do in order to do it.

190
00:12:56.916 --> 00:12:57.277
Yeah.

191
00:12:57.277 --> 00:12:58.000
Yeah.

192
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.103
<v ARSENEAULT>Who would usually pay for like the dress or—

193
00:13:01.103 --> 00:13:02.138
<v PAYNE>Usually the bride.

194
00:13:02.138 --> 00:13:06.276
In my sister’s case, she bought her own dress and whatever else.

195
00:13:06.276 --> 00:13:09.379
And my family helped with the wedding and whatever.

196
00:13:09.379 --> 00:13:13.172
I can’t say that, because I slipped off and got married.

197
00:13:13.172 --> 00:13:18.000
I went to Texas and got married by a justice of the peace. [laughs]

198
00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:18.690
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s cheaper.

199
00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:19.724
<v PAYNE>Yes, it was.

200
00:13:19.724 --> 00:13:21.103
<v ARSENEAULT>My brother did that.

201
00:13:21.103 --> 00:13:25.586
<v PAYNE>Later on, and it was years and years later, I had another wedding.

202
00:13:25.586 --> 00:13:29.724
And I had it here in this house after we moved back.

203
00:13:29.724 --> 00:13:31.103
And family all came.

204
00:13:31.103 --> 00:13:39.034
This was kind of like a meeting place for the whole family when, and not just my family, but the Payne’s family also.

205
00:13:39.034 --> 00:13:44.207
And we just felt like, my husband, he’s deceased, that the lord had blessed us.

206
00:13:44.207 --> 00:13:45.931
We’ve moved away to California.

207
00:13:45.931 --> 00:13:48.000
And I didn’t want to go.

208
00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.414
I think I cried for two weeks.

209
00:13:50.414 --> 00:13:55.931
And he said, “Well, I think I can provide us with a better life over there.

210
00:13:55.931 --> 00:13:56.276
So—

211
00:13:56.276 --> 00:13:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>This was your husband?

212
00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.092
<v PAYNE>This was, yeah.

213
00:13:59.092 --> 00:14:08.917
So I loaded all of the little junk – that’s a picture of us up there – loaded up all the little junk -- and I had a cousin that,

214
00:14:08.917 --> 00:14:15.103
and this, again, shows you family – I had a cousin that had just graduated from high school.

215
00:14:15.103 --> 00:14:24.200
And he and I left from here, pulling one of the largest U-Haul trailers that you could and [hitched it to] a ’67 Mustang, okay?

216
00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.747
And that’s how we went to California.

217
00:14:26.747 --> 00:14:28.203
But back to this.

218
00:14:28.203 --> 00:14:31.114
This kind of became like a meeting place.

219
00:14:31.114 --> 00:14:35.117
If it was a birthday, usually we had birthdays over here.

220
00:14:35.117 --> 00:14:37.664
Christmas holidays, Christmas over here or whatever.

221
00:14:37.664 --> 00:14:46.033
Then as the family got bigger, we would always cook a dish and meet at somebody else’s house and we tried rotating it.

222
00:14:46.033 --> 00:14:51.492
And it just wasn’t my family that did it, it was my husband’s family, also.

223
00:14:51.492 --> 00:14:57.314
And if we went to my parents then for early Christmas for lunch or whatever else,

224
00:14:57.314 --> 00:15:02.044
we always went to his family for dinner that evening or whatever else.

225
00:15:02.044 --> 00:15:04.228
But we always made the rounds.

226
00:15:04.228 --> 00:15:09.322
And it happened with everybody that whatever was going on, whatever holiday that was,

227
00:15:09.322 --> 00:15:14.053
you managed to touch base or to visit somebody at that particular time.

228
00:15:14.053 --> 00:15:17.692
And like I said, we all put in for food.

229
00:15:17.692 --> 00:15:20.603
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the other holiday traditions?

230
00:15:20.603 --> 00:15:24.969
So you would like have lunch with one family and then dinner—

231
00:15:24.969 --> 00:15:32.611
<v PAYNE>The other holiday tradition was every New Year’s, I mean, every Christmas Eve, we opened Christmas presents at my sister’s home.

232
00:15:32.611 --> 00:15:37.342
And that was because she had the youngest child at that particular time.

233
00:15:37.342 --> 00:15:40.981
And then we would always have Christmas dinner over here.

234
00:15:40.981 --> 00:15:43.892
We would always have Thanksgiving dinner at Mom’s.

235
00:15:43.892 --> 00:15:46.439
So that way, all of the major

236
00:15:46.439 --> 00:15:47.531
<c.p>holidays was covered.

237
00:15:47.531 --> 00:15:49.714
And we took care of that.

238
00:15:49.714 --> 00:15:57.719
New Year’s Eve or Christmas we had a big pot of gumbo or whatever else and that would take care of that.

239
00:15:57.719 --> 00:16:02.814
And then for Christmas, one would get a turkey, one would get a ham.

240
00:16:02.814 --> 00:16:08.272
And by the time we got together, we had more food than we could eat.

241
00:16:08.272 --> 00:16:09.000
Yeah.

242
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.188
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have like a signature dish or anything that you would always make?

243
00:16:15.188 --> 00:16:22.260
<v PAYNE>My sister’s signature dish, which I don’t care for that very much, a spinach casserole, okay?

244
00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:23.144
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s that?

245
00:16:23.144 --> 00:16:30.657
<v PAYNE>She has spinach, Velveeta cheese, and it’s something, I don't know if it’s with jalapenos or whatever,

246
00:16:30.657 --> 00:16:35.077
but it has a little spiciness to it and whatever.

247
00:16:35.077 --> 00:16:41.265
I mean, it’s a very delicious dish, but I’m not a big spinach eater.

248
00:16:41.265 --> 00:16:47.895
Mom’s thing, you cannot beat her when it comes to banana pudding and potato pie.

249
00:16:47.895 --> 00:16:54.525
She makes her banana puddings from scratch and use the egg white for the topping.

250
00:16:54.525 --> 00:16:58.061
And they come out perfect all the time.

251
00:16:58.061 --> 00:16:59.829
<v ARSENEAULT>That sounds really good.

252
00:16:59.829 --> 00:17:08.669
<v PAYNE>And it’s a toss-up between a cousin of mine and me of who can get to the banana pudding first.

253
00:17:08.669 --> 00:17:13.530
Her potato pies look just like a yam before it’s cooked.

254
00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:14.856
They never change

255
00:17:14.856 --> 00:17:20.602
<c.p>the color because she doesn’t use a lot of cinnamon or whatever else.

256
00:17:20.602 --> 00:17:21.486
They’re delicious.

257
00:17:21.486 --> 00:17:25.022
I can eat a whole pie by myself.

258
00:17:25.022 --> 00:17:27.232
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s a sweet potato pie?

259
00:17:27.232 --> 00:17:29.000
<v PAYNE>Sweet potato pie.

260
00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.947
<v ARSENEAULT>And she makes it in like a pie crust.

261
00:17:31.947 --> 00:17:33.257
<v PAYNE>In a pie crust.

262
00:17:33.257 --> 00:17:35.221
<v ARSENEAULT>And you eat a whole pie?

263
00:17:35.221 --> 00:17:42.752
<v PAYNE>I can eat a whole, not all at one time, but in the course of one day I can eat a whole pie.

264
00:17:42.752 --> 00:17:47.336
Grandmother Eunice [her father’s mother], which was her mom, one of her specialty was,

265
00:17:47.336 --> 00:17:53.885
and you will probably never hear of this before, and Dad has learned to mimic it, an Irish potato pie.

266
00:17:53.885 --> 00:17:59.451
You make it just like a sweet potato pie, but apparently you have to put more sugar.

267
00:17:59.451 --> 00:18:04.690
Because a sweet potato has some natural sugars to it, but the Irish potato pie doesn’t.

268
00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:07.965
And you make it the same way in a crust.

269
00:18:07.965 --> 00:18:12.221
And you can put the little crusty strips on top, if you want.

270
00:18:12.221 --> 00:18:13.858
Or just leave it open.

271
00:18:13.858 --> 00:18:14.186
Delicious.

272
00:18:14.186 --> 00:18:14.841
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds good.

273
00:18:14.841 --> 00:18:16.478
<v PAYNE>I can eat that, too.

274
00:18:16.478 --> 00:18:19.097
And like I said, mine is usually meats.

275
00:18:19.097 --> 00:18:24.336
And my husband taught me how to cook. [laughs] He came from a very large family,

276
00:18:24.336 --> 00:18:30.230
and I guess he had helped share in responsibilities of getting things done in the kitchen and whatever.

277
00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:34.814
As far as seasonings, my family didn’t use a lot of seasonings and whatever.

278
00:18:34.814 --> 00:18:36.124
And his family did.

279
00:18:36.124 --> 00:18:38.088
And somehow or another, they’ve merged.

280
00:18:38.088 --> 00:18:42.345
Because now we both use more seasoning than what they used to use.

281
00:18:42.345 --> 00:18:43.000
Yeah.

282
00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.695
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he like the oldest of his family?

283
00:18:45.695 --> 00:18:47.379
Or in the middle somewhere?

284
00:18:47.379 --> 00:18:52.768
<v PAYNE>He had a half-brother that was older than him, but he was the oldest male sibling.

285
00:18:52.768 --> 00:18:56.474
That man could get up at two o’clock in the morning.

286
00:18:56.474 --> 00:19:03.884
And if something was frozen in the freezer that he wanted to eat, he would thaw it out and start cooking it.

287
00:19:03.884 --> 00:19:04.221
Yeah.

288
00:19:04.221 --> 00:19:04.895
Very independent.

289
00:19:04.895 --> 00:19:07.926
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s nice to have a man who can cook.

290
00:19:07.926 --> 00:19:08.600
<v PAYNE>Yes, yes.

291
00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:09.947
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s on my list.

292
00:19:09.947 --> 00:19:10.284
<v PAYNE>Okay.

293
00:19:10.284 --> 00:19:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about your home and your neighbors’ homes growing up?

294
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:21.400
<v PAYNE>My early, early, I came to move in Mossville when I was about eight, nine years old.

295
00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:22.529
I’m seventy-one now.

296
00:19:22.529 --> 00:19:26.294
We lived, I want to say it’s Rigmaiden [Smith] Street.

297
00:19:26.294 --> 00:19:32.318
Because we rented a small house that was right on the corner from Dad’s Uncle Leo.

298
00:19:32.318 --> 00:19:40.600
And we stayed there for a couple of years until we moved here [to Perkins Avenue, where her parents built a home].

299
00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:45.118
But when we were over there, across the street was the Franks.

300
00:19:45.118 --> 00:19:50.012
And one of their daughters, Maybelle, became a very good friend of mine.

301
00:19:50.012 --> 00:19:51.518
Oh, got a fly.

302
00:19:51.518 --> 00:19:54.906
And then a little further down was the Vincents.

303
00:19:54.906 --> 00:19:59.800
And they had a daughter, Lucille, and she became a friend of mine.

304
00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:06.953
And we could walk to the swimming pool in the summer and things like that because we were close.

305
00:20:06.953 --> 00:20:15.612
We didn’t have to worry about child molestation or somebody coming along and harming us because everybody looked out for each other’s kids.

306
00:20:15.612 --> 00:20:22.012
Some adult always saw you and knew what you were doing, all the step of the way.

307
00:20:22.012 --> 00:20:28.412
So we could go to the rec and we could hang around at the rec and whatever.

308
00:20:28.412 --> 00:20:32.176
And on the rec site was the old school site.

309
00:20:32.176 --> 00:20:38.953
It was two little framed wooden buildings, and that’s where we went to school until Mossville opened up.

310
00:20:38.953 --> 00:20:45.729
And at Mossville, I guess I was so oblivious, I didn’t realize until we started doing this history,

311
00:20:45.729 --> 00:20:49.494
that Ms. Rogers that was really her first teaching job.

312
00:20:49.494 --> 00:20:57.400
We had so much respect for them, even though some of them was only four or five years older than us,

313
00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:03.424
we didn’t realize that at the time, that they had no problem with discipline or whatever.

314
00:21:03.424 --> 00:21:10.576
Because we knew when you walked in Ms. Rogers’ room, get your books out and get started, you know?

315
00:21:10.576 --> 00:21:15.471
And it was the same thing with Ms. West or Ms. Spikes or

316
00:21:15.471 --> 00:21:17.353
<c.p>whomever that was teaching us.

317
00:21:17.353 --> 00:21:19.612
But we had that camaraderie there.

318
00:21:19.612 --> 00:21:23.000
Thomas is—that fly just won’t leave us alone.

319
00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:24.064
<v ARSENEAULT>I know. [laughs]

320
00:21:24.064 --> 00:21:30.091
<v PAYNE>Thomas Porter’s family, when we were in high school, would always give like parties or whatever else.

321
00:21:30.091 --> 00:21:35.410
And we could go to those parties, and usually they were like on a Friday—no,

322
00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:41.438
they had to have been on a Sunday night because it must have been in the summertime.

323
00:21:41.438 --> 00:21:48.175
And I can remember, because I was so embarrassed, Mom and her sister had worked together at Maplewood Nursery.

324
00:21:48.175 --> 00:21:57.393
So after church had closed and they were through babysitting, she was just going to casually drop by to see if I wanted a ride home.

325
00:21:57.393 --> 00:22:03.776
And I was embarrassed for her to come pick me up in front of my teenage friends [laughs].

326
00:22:03.776 --> 00:22:12.285
And then when we moved over here [years earlier in the 1950s], we had a neighbor, they were the LeDets, Kelly and Clemonce LeDet.

327
00:22:12.285 --> 00:22:16.186
And Lois [their daughter] and I became very, very good friends.

328
00:22:16.186 --> 00:22:20.795
So we went to record hops and any other activities that they had.

329
00:22:20.795 --> 00:22:26.823
At that time, he was a Lutcher, Nelly Lutcher’s brother, Bubba Lutcher, he would have record hops.

330
00:22:26.823 --> 00:22:29.659
And we could go to those record hops.

331
00:22:29.659 --> 00:22:36.042
And again, because Lois had a brother that was about a year and a half younger than her,

332
00:22:36.042 --> 00:22:40.296
which was a teenager, also, it was like he was our chaperone.

333
00:22:40.296 --> 00:22:45.970
And he would have to go along with us and whatever else whenever we went someplace.

334
00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.452
But like I said, we had freedom.

335
00:22:48.452 --> 00:22:51.288
We could just about go and do anything.

336
00:22:51.288 --> 00:22:56.607
And we were never bad kids that really got in trouble or anything like that.

337
00:22:56.607 --> 00:23:00.507
So I guess our parents just gave us some extra [trust].

338
00:23:00.507 --> 00:23:07.244
During the summer, when I was about fourteen to fifteen years old, I would end up babysitting my cousins,

339
00:23:07.244 --> 00:23:11.853
which I hated at the time, because I felt that was ruining my

340
00:23:11.853 --> 00:23:12.208
<c.p>summer.

341
00:23:12.208 --> 00:23:14.335
But it wasn’t a full day.

342
00:23:14.335 --> 00:23:23.199
It was usually from like maybe eight in the morning till about one o’clock or something like that until my aunt got back from work.

343
00:23:23.199 --> 00:23:31.000
And I guess she compensated for it because she didn’t know it at the time, but I didn’t like that, also.

344
00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:36.895
Every year before school started, two or three weeks before, we had a store called J.J.

345
00:23:36.895 --> 00:23:38.368
Newberry’s in Lake Charles.

346
00:23:38.368 --> 00:23:46.474
Mom would go and she would pick out five pieces of cotton material with some type of print or something in it,

347
00:23:46.474 --> 00:23:49.421
and buy five blouses that matched that print.

348
00:23:49.421 --> 00:23:58.263
And she would bring that material to my Aunt Alean and she would sew and make me skirts to start school that particular year.

349
00:23:58.263 --> 00:24:03.789
I know I was blessed now because, like I said, times were pretty hard then.

350
00:24:03.789 --> 00:24:07.842
But they always made sure that we had or whatever else.

351
00:24:07.842 --> 00:24:13.000
And we laughed as we grew older because my Aunt Alean had six kids,

352
00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:19.263
where Mom only had me for a number of years and then my two sisters came along.

353
00:24:19.263 --> 00:24:25.895
So we always seemed to have a little more meat in our meals than what Aunt Alean had.

354
00:24:25.895 --> 00:24:32.158
And we were laughing the other day and I told my cousin after her mom had passed,

355
00:24:32.158 --> 00:24:36.579
I said, “I cooked a big pot of white beans and sausage.

356
00:24:36.579 --> 00:24:39.895
Would you like for me to bring you some?”

357
00:24:39.895 --> 00:24:40.263
“No!

358
00:24:40.263 --> 00:24:45.789
I ate enough beans when I was growing up, I don’t want any more beans.

359
00:24:45.789 --> 00:24:51.316
No thank you!” [laughs] And even today, you know, we’re adults, we still live basically.

360
00:24:51.316 --> 00:24:59.789
We have a couple of cousins that live in the Houston area or whatever, but they still come back and forth and whatever.

361
00:24:59.789 --> 00:25:03.842
But we still, you know, are very close to each other.

362
00:25:03.842 --> 00:25:09.000
My cousin called me yesterday and, “they’re still in the trunk of my vehicle,

363
00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:14.526
I have some plastic containers, if you need them to move come pick them up.”

364
00:25:14.526 --> 00:25:18.211
And so I went over and I picked them up.

365
00:25:18.211 --> 00:25:23.737
Whatever, I’m very blessed to have been put in the family that I’m put in.

366
00:25:23.737 --> 00:25:30.368
Whenever there is a need, and if one of the others have it, we have each other’s back.

367
00:25:30.368 --> 00:25:37.000
And there’s no, “Well, I did this for you,” or “I did that for you” or whatever.

368
00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:38.148
<v ARSENEAULT>It doesn’t matter.

369
00:25:38.148 --> 00:25:39.679
<v PAYNE>It just doesn’t exist.

370
00:25:39.679 --> 00:25:40.827
It doesn’t matter.

371
00:25:40.827 --> 00:25:41.592
It doesn’t.

372
00:25:41.592 --> 00:25:45.801
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you all used to do at the record hops?

373
00:25:45.801 --> 00:25:51.541
<v PAYNE>We had those skirts with those big old can cans under them that frilled out.

374
00:25:51.541 --> 00:25:53.071
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a poodle skirt?

375
00:25:53.071 --> 00:25:54.985
<v PAYNE>Yeah, like the poodle skirts.

376
00:25:54.985 --> 00:26:04.168
And one of the records that I remember is Chubby Checker’s “Let’s Twist Again Like We Did Last Summer.” [laughs] So we would dance.

377
00:26:04.168 --> 00:26:10.673
And you didn’t really have to worry about too much about anybody hanging out on the outside.

378
00:26:10.673 --> 00:26:14.883
Because the teachers was there and they chaperoned it fairly close.

379
00:26:14.883 --> 00:26:23.684
And if there was a problem or something, we knew that our parents would know about it by the time we got home.

380
00:26:23.684 --> 00:26:27.893
So if you didn’t want to get punished, that was it.

381
00:26:27.893 --> 00:26:33.633
And there were times, especially during the summer, when we had them almost every weekend.

382
00:26:33.633 --> 00:26:39.372
And oh, before you get to Moss Avenue, there was another little place, Ms. Martill’s.

383
00:26:39.372 --> 00:26:45.112
She had a little canteen and we could go there on Sunday afternoons or whenever.

384
00:26:45.112 --> 00:26:52.000
And there was a jukebox that you could play records from and you could dance in there.

385
00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:53.835
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it in her house?

386
00:26:53.835 --> 00:26:56.404
<v PAYNE>She lived in one part of it.

387
00:26:56.404 --> 00:27:00.440
Then she had this room, probably a little bigger than this.

388
00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:05.578
It seemed bigger with me being a child, but it probably wasn’t that big.

389
00:27:05.578 --> 00:27:12.917
And she had a few little tables and stuff in there that you could sit down and spend time with.

390
00:27:12.917 --> 00:27:18.055
And then right across the street from the rec, they tore that building down.

391
00:27:18.055 --> 00:27:19.890
That was Ms. Lula Lyon’s.

392
00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:25.761
And she had a little canteen that we could go to and we could hang out.

393
00:27:25.761 --> 00:27:28.330
And there was one other one, also.

394
00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:28.530
They had Ms. Martill’s, they had Ms. Lula Lyons—

395
00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[. . . ?]

396
00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:36.036
<v PAYNE>And I’m pretty sure there was another one [in Bel Air].

397
00:27:36.036 --> 00:27:41.907
And Lola, which was a Rigmaiden, Josh Rigmaiden and them [daughter of Josh and Noreen Rigmaiden],

398
00:27:41.907 --> 00:27:47.044
Lola and them would have snow cones, and you could go buy snow cones.

399
00:27:47.044 --> 00:27:51.448
And then the ice cream truck passed pretty frequent at that time.

400
00:27:51.448 --> 00:28:00.254
If you had a little money, you could buy you an ice cream cone and stuff like that during the summer to cool off.

401
00:28:00.254 --> 00:28:02.823
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there an ice cream shop anywhere?

402
00:28:02.823 --> 00:28:03.923
<v PAYNE>Not per say.

403
00:28:03.923 --> 00:28:10.895
At the canteens, it was more like beverages, sodas that you could buy and stuff like that and all.

404
00:28:10.895 --> 00:28:17.867
Later on, this would have been probably, because I remember, Prater Road used to flood before it became blacktop.

405
00:28:17.867 --> 00:28:23.738
And after a big, big rain, we would get out there and we would go wading.

406
00:28:23.738 --> 00:28:30.343
You couldn’t pay me to get out there now when I think about all the snakes and everything.

407
00:28:30.343 --> 00:28:32.911
We never even thought about those things.

408
00:28:32.911 --> 00:28:39.516
I mean, that was fun to get out there and just get wet and play in that water.

409
00:28:39.516 --> 00:28:41.718
Well, there was a merchant that

410
00:28:41.718 --> 00:28:42.085
<c.p>came.

411
00:28:42.085 --> 00:28:43.185
He was white.

412
00:28:43.185 --> 00:28:44.653
His name was Rougeau.

413
00:28:44.653 --> 00:28:47.222
And he had a grocery store there.

414
00:28:47.222 --> 00:28:52.726
And this would have been, like I said, in the ‘50s, late ‘50s or whatever.

415
00:28:52.726 --> 00:28:55.294
And we could purchase almost anything there.

416
00:28:55.294 --> 00:29:03.000
He had groceries and he had little ice cream beverages and stuff like that that you could purchase from him.

417
00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:04.776
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he live in Mossville?

418
00:29:04.776 --> 00:29:06.553
<v PAYNE>Yeah, he lived in Mossville.

419
00:29:06.553 --> 00:29:15.789
And even before that, years and years ago, by the railroad track, on the opposite side toward Westlake [Louisiana], there was a man named Ira Garrett.

420
00:29:15.789 --> 00:29:20.408
He had a store and that was also part of a canteen, also.

421
00:29:20.408 --> 00:29:28.934
And really, he had the first post office that was in this particular area that you could use to mail your letters and all.

422
00:29:28.934 --> 00:29:32.132
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that where the original post office was located?

423
00:29:32.132 --> 00:29:33.553
<v PAYNE>I’m thinking it was.

424
00:29:33.553 --> 00:29:36.750
That he was the one who had had that.

425
00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:37.105
Yeah.

426
00:29:37.105 --> 00:29:39.592
<v ARSENEAULT>I know there was one in 1915.

427
00:29:39.592 --> 00:29:47.763
<v PAYNE>I’m thinking it carried on because with me growing up as a child, he was an old man when I was a teenager.

428
00:29:47.763 --> 00:29:50.250
So more than likely, it possibly was.

429
00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:57.000
Or it was a member of his family who had had it and he just kept it open.

430
00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:02.176
<v ARSENEAULT>So that was probably, in Mr. Garrett’s, that was the original.

431
00:30:02.176 --> 00:30:02.647
<v PAYNE>Original.

432
00:30:02.647 --> 00:30:03.118
Yeah.

433
00:30:03.118 --> 00:30:04.059
I’d say.

434
00:30:04.059 --> 00:30:05.941
<v ARSENEAULT>What was he like?

435
00:30:05.941 --> 00:30:10.647
<v PAYNE>In a child’s eye, kind of a burly type man.

436
00:30:10.647 --> 00:30:12.059
Medium brown skin.

437
00:30:12.059 --> 00:30:16.294
Hair was already more gray than salt and pepper.

438
00:30:16.294 --> 00:30:19.118
Kind of burly chest type person.

439
00:30:19.118 --> 00:30:19.588
Quiet.

440
00:30:19.588 --> 00:30:27.118
We knew not to give him any trouble, though. [laughs] But he made us feel welcome.

441
00:30:27.118 --> 00:30:35.118
And it wasn’t like we were a pest when we went over there or anything like that.

442
00:30:35.118 --> 00:30:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did it close?

443
00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:41.706
<v PAYNE>I would say probably, I left from here in ’69.

444
00:30:41.706 --> 00:30:46.412
So I would say sometimes in the’60s, he probably stopped.

445
00:30:46.412 --> 00:30:52.059
And I don't know if it was due to death or whatever.

446
00:30:52.059 --> 00:30:54.412
I can’t recall back then.

447
00:30:54.412 --> 00:31:01.000
But it would have been somewhere in the ‘60s that he stopped that.

448
00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:05.022
<v ARSENEAULT>What are some of your earliest childhood memories of Mossville?

449
00:31:05.022 --> 00:31:09.446
We’ve kind of talked about it a little bit already, but—

450
00:31:09.446 --> 00:31:14.674
<v PAYNE>Being able to go in the backyard, over in our neighbors, and play.

451
00:31:14.674 --> 00:31:18.696
It [their rental] was a little white wood frame house.

452
00:31:18.696 --> 00:31:22.315
Had a small porch in the front of it.

453
00:31:22.315 --> 00:31:24.326
Little living area and whatever.

454
00:31:24.326 --> 00:31:30.761
Couldn’t have been no more than three rooms: kitchen, a living area and that [one bedroom].

455
00:31:30.761 --> 00:31:35.587
So I probably ended up sleeping in the living area or whatever.

456
00:31:35.587 --> 00:31:42.826
Then like I say, I was about ten, eleven, when we moved over here [to 4019 Perkins Avenue].

457
00:31:42.826 --> 00:31:45.239
And it was a big deal.

458
00:31:45.239 --> 00:31:45.641
A

459
00:31:45.641 --> 00:31:47.652
<c.p>lot of this was woods.

460
00:31:47.652 --> 00:31:49.663
All of this was wood.

461
00:31:49.663 --> 00:31:53.685
They had the house on the corner that’s still there.

462
00:31:53.685 --> 00:31:54.891
It was green.

463
00:31:54.891 --> 00:32:01.728
Then a vacant lot, my parents, another vacant lot, and then the LeDets, then another vacant lot,

464
00:32:01.728 --> 00:32:09.370
and a little man, [Nolan] June Braxton that was related to the man that I bought the land from.

465
00:32:09.370 --> 00:32:10.576
They were brothers.

466
00:32:10.576 --> 00:32:15.000
And he had a little trailer that he lived in.

467
00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:22.292
And eventually, and I can say a couple of years later, my aunt and them moved there [Moss Avenue].

468
00:32:22.292 --> 00:32:27.666
And there was one older house that’s tore down where that two-story house is.

469
00:32:27.666 --> 00:32:31.888
And that was the only homes that was in this area.

470
00:32:31.888 --> 00:32:34.958
And we could roam all over the neighborhood.

471
00:32:34.958 --> 00:32:37.261
And we could play or whatever.

472
00:32:37.261 --> 00:32:39.948
We even went crawfishing in the ditches.

473
00:32:39.948 --> 00:32:46.856
We didn’t have where you could go buy crawfish at a market and have all of those things.

474
00:32:46.856 --> 00:32:54.916
We were lucky if we caught fifteen to twenty crawfish, and we were ready to go cook them when we did.

475
00:32:54.916 --> 00:32:57.219
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you all cook them?

476
00:32:57.219 --> 00:32:57.987
<v PAYNE>Fry them.

477
00:32:57.987 --> 00:32:58.755
Boil them.

478
00:32:58.755 --> 00:33:01.441
We really did a number on them.

479
00:33:01.441 --> 00:33:06.047
We would boil them to kill them, pour hot water on them.

480
00:33:06.047 --> 00:33:06.815
Peel them.

481
00:33:06.815 --> 00:33:13.339
And then put them in a skillet with a little grease and then kind of sauté them.

482
00:33:13.339 --> 00:33:13.723
Yeah.

483
00:33:13.723 --> 00:33:15.642
But that was good eating.

484
00:33:15.642 --> 00:33:24.470
A little fishing pole made out of like a little tree limb, a little tiny, about as big as my finger or smaller,

485
00:33:24.470 --> 00:33:31.762
with a string on it and a little piece of meat tied to the end of it, do that.

486
00:33:31.762 --> 00:33:39.822
Some of my best, best childhood memories was just before we moved here, which we stayed in the Willow Springs area.

487
00:33:39.822 --> 00:33:40.974
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that close?

488
00:33:40.974 --> 00:33:44.044
<v PAYNE>Maybe like twelve, fifteen minutes from here, okay?

489
00:33:44.044 --> 00:33:50.569
And that part of my family is related to the Perkins and the Vincents that lived here.

490
00:33:50.569 --> 00:33:54.023
I was probably five years old at that time.

491
00:33:54.023 --> 00:33:56.326
Mom was living in Dequincy [Louisiana].

492
00:33:56.326 --> 00:34:04.386
And just as you go into the bend in Dequincy, we lived in a home over on the left hand side.

493
00:34:04.386 --> 00:34:07.841
And she had gone out to buy some fruit.

494
00:34:07.841 --> 00:34:10.911
So she told me, stay on the porch.

495
00:34:10.911 --> 00:34:13.214
Needless to say, I didn’t listen.

496
00:34:13.214 --> 00:34:14.366
I followed her.

497
00:34:14.366 --> 00:34:16.668
I got hit by a car.

498
00:34:16.668 --> 00:34:24.345
And I end up having a fractured leg, I think it was broken in two places, a concussion and whatever.

499
00:34:24.345 --> 00:34:30.486
Well, needless to say, I had to wear a full body cast with both hips in.

500
00:34:30.486 --> 00:34:33.172
Little hole cut to use the bathroom.

501
00:34:33.172 --> 00:34:37.778
This was the [left] leg that was broken all the way down.

502
00:34:37.778 --> 00:34:42.000
I had to wear that for about a year, okay?

503
00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:49.843
Well, while I was recuperating, they had this lady that would lose her mind from time to time [Lavenia “Bean” Portor].

504
00:34:49.843 --> 00:34:53.205
And on one particular occasion she lost her mind.

505
00:34:53.205 --> 00:35:00.675
And I remember about that time I was staying with my grandmother [Sophie Vincent] because I had broken my leg.

506
00:35:00.675 --> 00:35:03.663
They had taken what was called bean poles.

507
00:35:03.663 --> 00:35:11.880
Used to take little, small, small oak trees and cut them and you’d make bean sticks for your beans to grow on.

508
00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:24.205
Well, Grandfather [Joseph Vincent] had cut them the length of the bottom window and had jammed them all through the window as a protective barrier so she couldn’t get in or whatever else.

509
00:35:24.205 --> 00:35:30.928
And I remember laying in bed and Mom had a shotgun across her legs as a protective device.

510
00:35:30.928 --> 00:35:31.301
Yeah.

511
00:35:31.301 --> 00:35:32.422
I remember that.

512
00:35:32.422 --> 00:35:38.398
And there was another man by the name of Walter Smith, after I could walk around.

513
00:35:38.398 --> 00:35:42.133
I had to learn how to walk all over again.

514
00:35:42.133 --> 00:35:46.988
After I could walk around, he had what he called a branding pen.

515
00:35:46.988 --> 00:35:54.831
And he was, I guess you could say, a very wealthy man in this day and time because he had cattle,

516
00:35:54.831 --> 00:36:00.060
he had horses, he had a sugar cane mill and all of those things.

517
00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:08.651
So because there was no other children and we had moved down here at that time, he was, well he and my grandfather

518
00:36:08.651 --> 00:36:09.771
<c.p>were my buddies.

519
00:36:09.771 --> 00:36:13.133
So they spent a lot of time with me.

520
00:36:13.133 --> 00:36:18.735
He would take me to his branding pen and set me on the top rail.

521
00:36:18.735 --> 00:36:22.470
And I would watch him take care of his animals.

522
00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:27.325
And sometimes at night, my grandfather and I would sit on the porch.

523
00:36:27.325 --> 00:36:31.434
And he taught me how to name the number of stars.

524
00:36:31.434 --> 00:36:37.410
And one of the things he taught me has stayed with me all through my life.

525
00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:39.651
He said, “Can you talk twice?”

526
00:36:39.651 --> 00:36:40.024
“Yeah.”

527
00:36:40.024 --> 00:36:43.386
Do you know what it means to talk twice?”

528
00:36:43.386 --> 00:36:43.759
Nope.”

529
00:36:43.759 --> 00:36:46.000
“Well, why did you say yeah?”

530
00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:47.494
“Because you asked me.”

531
00:36:47.494 --> 00:36:57.205
He said, “When you talk twice that means you can say the same thing to whoever you have to say it to exactly the same way.

532
00:36:57.205 --> 00:36:59.072
And you don’t change it.

533
00:36:59.072 --> 00:37:05.422
You don’t change it to please this person, and you don’t change it to please that person.

534
00:37:05.422 --> 00:37:07.663
You repeat it the same way.

535
00:37:07.663 --> 00:37:11.398
And those values have stayed with me all my life.

536
00:37:11.398 --> 00:37:12.518
All my life.

537
00:37:12.518 --> 00:37:15.506
But I feel I had a great childhood.

538
00:37:15.506 --> 00:37:17.000
I really did.

539
00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were some of your childhood role models?

540
00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:22.500
Besides your grandfather?

541
00:37:22.500 --> 00:37:23.000
<v PAYNE>Mom.

542
00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.000
She was very stern.

543
00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:30.500
But she was a giving, and still is, a giving person.

544
00:37:30.500 --> 00:37:31.500
Strong will.

545
00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:32.500
Hard worker.

546
00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:36.000
And I admired those things about her.

547
00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:41.500
And I patterned myself after her in a lot of aspects.

548
00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:51.500
To show you how she is, last Thanksgiving my refrigerator went out so I had to go buy a refrigerator.

549
00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:56.500
So I decided, okay, I’m going to charge this booger.

550
00:37:56.500 --> 00:38:00.500
I’m not going to pay cash for it.

551
00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:06.000
She snooped around and found out where I had charged it.

552
00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:14.500
And when I opened my Christmas [card], I didn’t get a Christmas present, I got an envelope.

553
00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:23.000
When I opened my card and I saw a receipt and I’m looking, saying, “What is this?”

554
00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:30.000
She had gone and she and Dad had paid 500 dollars on my refrigerator.

555
00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.500
That was my Christmas present.

556
00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:35.000
And she’s taught us that.

557
00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:39.500
She taught us to look out for one another.

558
00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:41.000
Voice our opinion.

559
00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:43.500
Strong, strong belief in the

560
00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:44.000
<c.p>lord.

561
00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:48.000
And saw to it we went to church.

562
00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:54.000
If we lived in her house, we had to go to church.

563
00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:59.000
And I lived there until I was nineteen years old.

564
00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:00.000
So.

565
00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.862
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you all go to church?

566
00:39:02.862 --> 00:39:11.447
<v PAYNE>My entire life I have gone, except when I lived in California, to Willow Springs, which is in North Sulphur [Louisiana].

567
00:39:11.447 --> 00:39:21.258
And when I was in California, I went to [Tribestone?] and the Reverend Reynolds was the pastor there, which was some thirty-seven years ago.

568
00:39:21.258 --> 00:39:22.893
So I admire her.

569
00:39:22.893 --> 00:39:23.302
Yeah.

570
00:39:23.302 --> 00:39:27.390
She just had a couple of minor surgeries and whatever.

571
00:39:27.390 --> 00:39:31.887
And she was diagnosed with leukemia probably some fifteen years ago.

572
00:39:31.887 --> 00:39:38.019
And in the first three years, she probably had two to three rounds of chemo.

573
00:39:38.019 --> 00:39:42.107
And has not had to have any chemo since then.

574
00:39:42.107 --> 00:39:43.742
So she’s a survivor.

575
00:39:43.742 --> 00:39:51.101
And she has told all of us that when it’s her time to go, she’s ready to go.

576
00:39:51.101 --> 00:40:00.094
She’s explained to us what she wants and what she doesn’t want, in reference to how she wants things taken care of.

577
00:40:00.094 --> 00:40:02.956
So I admire her for those things.

578
00:40:02.956 --> 00:40:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How old is she?

579
00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:05.444
<v PAYNE>Eighty-eight.

580
00:40:05.444 --> 00:40:09.444
Just came back from Disney about three weeks ago.

581
00:40:09.444 --> 00:40:10.778
Let’s see, yeah.

582
00:40:10.778 --> 00:40:14.333
I admire, I admire both of my parents.

583
00:40:14.333 --> 00:40:15.667
Daddy’s my stepfather.

584
00:40:15.667 --> 00:40:23.667
I had Aunt Tommy, his sister, will argue with anybody who contradicts that I am not a Prater.

585
00:40:23.667 --> 00:40:27.222
“Yes, she is, that was my first grand-niece.”

586
00:40:27.222 --> 00:40:28.556
Etcetera, etcetera, etcetera.

587
00:40:28.556 --> 00:40:31.222
They have a close family, also.

588
00:40:31.222 --> 00:40:39.667
And that was another place growing up as a child, we always went to Grandma Eunice’s for, both of

589
00:40:39.667 --> 00:40:47.222
<c.p>my grandparents, but we always went to Grandma Eunice’s for Christmas, Thanksgiving, New Year’s, all of that.

590
00:40:47.222 --> 00:40:50.778
And that’s when she had those potato pies.

591
00:40:50.778 --> 00:40:55.667
And we laugh because that woman was deadly with a dishcloth.

592
00:40:55.667 --> 00:41:05.000
She could have her back turned to you and if you did something else, I never felt the wrath of it.

593
00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:13.000
But kids growing up, she rolled it up real quick and got you real quick. [laughs] She did.

594
00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:13.444
Yeah.

595
00:41:13.444 --> 00:41:17.000
Oh, that’s another thing, too, I remember.

596
00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:20.574
I don't know how in the world they made it.

597
00:41:20.574 --> 00:41:28.078
I tell you, their home was destroyed when Hurricane Audrey, no, Rita and Katrina came through, that’s when it was destroyed.

598
00:41:28.078 --> 00:41:29.508
Had a front porch.

599
00:41:29.508 --> 00:41:31.652
It was a wood frame house.

600
00:41:31.652 --> 00:41:37.727
And I have a piece of the wood in there now from Mama and Papa Prater’s home.

601
00:41:37.727 --> 00:41:40.944
A wood frame house with a small front porch.

602
00:41:40.944 --> 00:41:44.160
When you walked in, that was the living area.

603
00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:47.019
Off, and it faced this way [Prater Road].

604
00:41:47.019 --> 00:41:51.307
So you walked in and you walked immediately into the living area.

605
00:41:51.307 --> 00:41:54.524
Off on the right hand side was the kitchen.

606
00:41:54.524 --> 00:41:59.527
From the living room you could go into another room, which was the bedroom.

607
00:41:59.527 --> 00:42:05.602
That was my grandparents’ bedroom, and they had a little small, small back porch off from it.

608
00:42:05.602 --> 00:42:13.821
Then they had another room – no hallways, now, just room to room to room – the next room would have been the boys’ room.

609
00:42:13.821 --> 00:42:17.752
And the room at the very end was the girls’ room.

610
00:42:17.752 --> 00:42:22.398
And there was double beds in both of the rooms on both sides.

611
00:42:22.398 --> 00:42:27.044
Closets was unheard of, basically, at that particular time, when they built that.

612
00:42:27.044 --> 00:42:33.119
Because you’ve got to think this was like in the ‘30s when they built this house, okay?

613
00:42:33.119 --> 00:42:39.552
And I can remember because Aunt Tommy and Aunt Bernice, when they were growing up, shared a bed.

614
00:42:39.552 --> 00:42:47.056
But by that time Aunt Bernice was a young woman, so she had already gotten married and moved away from home.

615
00:42:47.056 --> 00:42:49.915
So Aunt Tommy had a bed by herself.

616
00:42:49.915 --> 00:42:54.204
But Irie and Ruby had to share a bed with each other.

617
00:42:54.204 --> 00:43:00.279
And they had, one bed’s in the corner over there, one bed’s in the corner over here.

618
00:43:00.279 --> 00:43:05.282
Then they had like a clothesline that was stretched across, almost over the bed.

619
00:43:05.282 --> 00:43:11.000
And that’s where they had to hang their clothes around on the side of it.

620
00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:12.726
<v ARSENEAULT>That was their closet?

621
00:43:12.726 --> 00:43:14.452
<v PAYNE>That was their closet.

622
00:43:14.452 --> 00:43:21.788
And was always taught, whatever you have, keep it clean and neat and take care of it.

623
00:43:21.788 --> 00:43:23.945
So they was good people.

624
00:43:23.945 --> 00:43:30.849
And Grandpa Earthy, as another childhood memory, we were still living in [Leo] Prater’s rent house.

625
00:43:30.849 --> 00:43:36.459
Every Saturday, rain or shine, he did get a car before he died.

626
00:43:36.459 --> 00:43:42.932
But every Saturday, come rain or shine, when I was about nine, ten years old,

627
00:43:42.932 --> 00:43:48.541
he would hitch his horse to his wagon and drive to Westlake [Louisiana].

628
00:43:48.541 --> 00:43:53.288
And there was a larger grocery store there, Mannigan and Carol’s.

629
00:43:53.288 --> 00:43:58.897
And that’s where he would purchase groceries for the family and come back.

630
00:43:58.897 --> 00:44:07.096
But in addition to that, he also had a garden where he would grow his own vegetables and all.

631
00:44:07.096 --> 00:44:14.000
So he worked on a job, then he also planted that garden until he retired.

632
00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:18.254
And then, you know, by that time he had gotten a car.

633
00:44:18.254 --> 00:44:20.026
He was big stuff then.

634
00:44:20.026 --> 00:44:22.153
But every Saturday [he made groceries].

635
00:44:22.153 --> 00:44:23.926
And Prater Road out here.

636
00:44:23.926 --> 00:44:30.661
I mean, Old Spanish Trail, East Burton, [Willette?], whatever they call it on the other side of Prater Road,

637
00:44:30.661 --> 00:44:38.460
used to be a hard-surfaced area with gravel on each side that was only large enough for one car to drive down.

638
00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:41.296
And if you met a car, you had,

639
00:44:41.296 --> 00:44:49.095
the driver’s side on the hard surface and the passenger’s side was on the gravel side on the other side of it,

640
00:44:49.095 --> 00:44:50.868
and that’s how you maneuvered.

641
00:44:50.868 --> 00:44:54.767
We also had a bus that came from Lake Charles [Louisiana].

642
00:44:54.767 --> 00:44:58.667
And I’m thinking it was twenty-five cents to ride the bus.

643
00:44:58.667 --> 00:45:07.529
And you could get on the bus and go to Lake Charles and shop or whatever, and then catch the bus and come back home.

644
00:45:07.529 --> 00:45:10.365
So as a teenager, we could do that.

645
00:45:10.365 --> 00:45:18.164
If one or two of us got together, our parents would let us go to Lake Charles and we could go shop.

646
00:45:18.164 --> 00:45:21.000
So we had a lot of fun.

647
00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:27.769
<v ARSENEAULT>I was going to ask you what some of the things that you enjoyed as a child.

648
00:45:27.769 --> 00:45:31.750
We kind of talked about it a little bit, but—

649
00:45:31.750 --> 00:45:41.704
<v PAYNE>Some of the things that I enjoyed most as a child, I think was the time that I spent with my grandfather Joseph, Mom’s dad.

650
00:45:41.704 --> 00:45:45.287
He died when I was probably twelve years old.

651
00:45:45.287 --> 00:45:57.231
But between the time I could remember . . . And I was very blessed that God gave me a memory that I can remember when I was three and four years old.

652
00:45:57.231 --> 00:46:01.213
And at first they wanted to say no, you can’t.

653
00:46:01.213 --> 00:46:04.000
I said, “I can prove it.”

654
00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:09.039
His dad, Ben Vincent, was bedridden when I was two or three years old.

655
00:46:09.039 --> 00:46:10.478
I would say three.

656
00:46:10.478 --> 00:46:11.558
Three to four.

657
00:46:11.558 --> 00:46:19.116
And every time I would go visit him, [he’d hand me a little?] box of raisins that was under his pillow.

658
00:46:19.116 --> 00:46:21.635
Their home had a fireplace in it.

659
00:46:21.635 --> 00:46:24.154
And it was only three rooms, okay?

660
00:46:24.154 --> 00:46:28.113
My Uncle Bo apparently had had polio, because he was crippled.

661
00:46:28.113 --> 00:46:39.630
And they managed to hang a rope from one of the beams in the ceiling and that’s how he would lift himself on his bed to maneuver himself around and whatever else.

662
00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:41.429
And he was an adult.

663
00:46:41.429 --> 00:46:46.828
He’d lived through, I don't know what age or whatever he may have contacted this.

664
00:46:46.828 --> 00:46:51.866
But I know that that had to have been what had happened to him.

665
00:46:51.866 --> 00:46:57.265
And then when my [great] grandfather Ben got sick, he was in the next room.

666
00:46:57.265 --> 00:47:01.584
And my grandfather Ben always had those little raisins under his pillow.

667
00:47:01.584 --> 00:47:06.982
And off to the head of his bed was like over there in that corner.

668
00:47:06.982 --> 00:47:13.460
So he could lay in that corner and face like this wall over here, there was a fireplace.

669
00:47:13.460 --> 00:47:15.979
And it was made out of clay.

670
00:47:15.979 --> 00:47:21.738
And every so often, they would have to get more clay and mix it with straw.

671
00:47:21.738 --> 00:47:29.296
And they made what was called cats and they would reline the fireplace for any cracks or anything that was gone.

672
00:47:29.296 --> 00:47:32.535
Then you walked out of that room into what

673
00:47:32.535 --> 00:47:36.494
<c.p>was like a porch that ran across both of those rooms.

674
00:47:36.494 --> 00:47:41.172
And that was where the kitchen was when you went into the kitchen.

675
00:47:41.172 --> 00:47:46.571
And that’s the view that I have in my mind of my grandmother, my great-grandmother.

676
00:47:46.571 --> 00:47:50.170
Standing at the kitchen stove, which was in the corner.

677
00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:53.049
And she had a long black dress on.

678
00:47:53.049 --> 00:47:57.368
And apparently she wore black a lot, because I was told that.

679
00:47:57.368 --> 00:47:58.807
And she was cooking.

680
00:47:58.807 --> 00:48:01.326
So I just really appreciate my childhood.

681
00:48:01.326 --> 00:48:02.046
I do.

682
00:48:02.046 --> 00:48:06.005
But those were people who were very, very influential to me.

683
00:48:06.005 --> 00:48:09.244
And like I said, Walter Smith, he would come.

684
00:48:09.244 --> 00:48:13.563
And he would have a horse and he would have a slide.

685
00:48:13.563 --> 00:48:24.000
And I would get on the slide and he would take me down to his branding pen and put me on the fence and I’d watch those things.

686
00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:28.950
<v ARSENEAULT>How were you expected to behave in front of adults?

687
00:48:28.950 --> 00:48:32.415
<v PAYNE>I can give you a prime example.

688
00:48:32.415 --> 00:48:39.345
My grandfather Joseph and grandmother Sophie had gone to visit a cousin of theirs.

689
00:48:39.345 --> 00:48:43.305
And she would have been Mom’s same age.

690
00:48:43.305 --> 00:48:46.770
But they had gone to visit her.

691
00:48:46.770 --> 00:48:49.245
And it was one night.

692
00:48:49.245 --> 00:48:59.145
And it was the distance, probably, from here to the other street over there, from where the two homes was.

693
00:48:59.145 --> 00:49:04.095
And we were walking through a grassy trail at night.

694
00:49:04.095 --> 00:49:06.570
I said, “Bye, Ms. Addy.”

695
00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:13.005
And before you know it, I had a backhand lick in my mouth.

696
00:49:13.005 --> 00:49:16.965
I thought I had done the right thing.

697
00:49:16.965 --> 00:49:21.915
I used her first name instead of her last name.

698
00:49:21.915 --> 00:49:24.885
And that’s why I got it.

699
00:49:24.885 --> 00:49:28.350
And you know what my grandfather did?

700
00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:33.795
He teased me about it until the day that he died.

701
00:49:33.795 --> 00:49:34.290
Yeah.

702
00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:44.685
“Sophie hit you in the mouth and I do believe I heard you pass gas.” [laughs] I said, “Grandpa, I didn’t!”

703
00:49:44.685 --> 00:49:46.665
“You sure you didn’t?”

704
00:49:46.665 --> 00:49:50.130
He was a jokester, I’m telling you.

705
00:49:50.130 --> 00:49:50.625
Yeah.

706
00:49:50.625 --> 00:49:52.110
He really was.

707
00:49:52.110 --> 00:49:57.060
I don't know if he ever whipped any of us.

708
00:49:57.060 --> 00:49:59.040
He never had to!

709
00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:00.030
<v ARSENEAULT>Your grandfather?

710
00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:02.010
<v PAYNE>He never had to.

711
00:50:02.010 --> 00:50:03.000
No.

712
00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:06.083
<v ARSENEAULT>Who mainly did the disciplining in your family?

713
00:50:06.083 --> 00:50:06.469
<v PAYNE>Mom.

714
00:50:06.469 --> 00:50:07.240
My grandmother.

715
00:50:07.240 --> 00:50:16.104
When Mom was growing up, if they had done a load of clothes and it was not done to her [grandmother Sophie’s] satisfactory,

716
00:50:16.104 --> 00:50:23.427
they could be hanging on the line, she did not tell them to go get them and rewash them.

717
00:50:23.427 --> 00:50:24.969
She went out herself.

718
00:50:24.969 --> 00:50:31.906
And they had dirt yards, not a stitch of grass in the yard, took the pins all off,

719
00:50:31.906 --> 00:50:38.458
and just laid them right down in the dirt and they had to start all over again.

720
00:50:38.458 --> 00:50:43.083
So you knew you had better do it right the first time.

721
00:50:43.083 --> 00:50:43.469
Yeah.

722
00:50:43.469 --> 00:50:47.708
And like I said, Mom has her way of disciplining, also.

723
00:50:47.708 --> 00:50:50.792
Not to that extreme or anything like that.

724
00:50:50.792 --> 00:50:56.188
And it’s the same thing I’ve tried to teach my children and my grandchildren.

725
00:50:56.188 --> 00:51:02.740
Your clothes belong one of three places: on your back, in your closet or the dirty clothes.

726
00:51:02.740 --> 00:51:11.604
Three things you cannot take away from a person: your relationship with your god, the family that you came from and an education.

727
00:51:11.604 --> 00:51:13.146
People cannot touch that.

728
00:51:13.146 --> 00:51:15.844
So just a strong sense of family.

729
00:51:15.844 --> 00:51:22.010
And like I said, I ended up marrying a guy who basically had the same thing.

730
00:51:22.010 --> 00:51:27.792
Because when we first got married and he had three or four of his brothers,

731
00:51:27.792 --> 00:51:33.188
Larry was one of them and all, all of us, we were all together.

732
00:51:33.188 --> 00:51:38.198
You know, the sisters-in-law and all the kids and whatever else it was.

733
00:51:38.198 --> 00:51:39.740
At picnics and barbecues.

734
00:51:39.740 --> 00:51:41.667
Wilford’s dad loved to barbecue.

735
00:51:41.667 --> 00:51:43.594
So it was always that.

736
00:51:43.594 --> 00:51:43.979
Yeah.

737
00:51:43.979 --> 00:51:45.906
His mom liked Bingo. [laughter]

738
00:51:45.906 --> 00:51:46.292
<v ARSENEAULT>Funny.

739
00:51:46.292 --> 00:51:47.448
So many questions.

740
00:51:47.448 --> 00:51:51.302
We’re probably not going to get to all of these.

741
00:51:51.302 --> 00:51:53.229
Did you attend the movies?

742
00:51:53.229 --> 00:51:54.000
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

743
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:56.083
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you all go?

744
00:51:56.083 --> 00:51:59.417
<v PAYNE>There was a movie, well, they had Paramount.

745
00:51:59.417 --> 00:52:08.167
And you know, at that particular time, the whites and the blacks couldn’t be together, so there was a separate part.

746
00:52:08.167 --> 00:52:16.083
But there was a movie house in North Lake Charles also that we could go to and attend movies.

747
00:52:16.083 --> 00:52:18.583
But not out here in Mossville.

748
00:52:18.583 --> 00:52:21.083
We didn’t have nothing like that.

749
00:52:21.083 --> 00:52:24.833
You had to go to Lake Charles for that.

750
00:52:24.833 --> 00:52:27.750
<v ARSENEAULT>So the theaters were segregated coming up.

751
00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:28.583
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

752
00:52:28.583 --> 00:52:31.917
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of music did you listen to—?

753
00:52:31.917 --> 00:52:33.583
<v PAYNE>Like I told you—

754
00:52:33.583 --> 00:52:34.417
<v ARSENEAULT>Billie Holliday?

755
00:52:34.417 --> 00:52:36.500
<v PAYNE>Not so much Billie Holliday.

756
00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:37.333
Chubby Checker—

757
00:52:37.333 --> 00:52:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, that’s right.

758
00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:40.182
<v PAYNE>Otis Redding.

759
00:52:40.182 --> 00:52:47.864
I can see him. [sings] “You ain’t nothing but a hound dog.” [laughs]

760
00:52:47.864 --> 00:52:48.455
<v ARSENEAULT>Elvis?

761
00:52:48.455 --> 00:52:52.000
<v PAYNE>We listened to a little Elvis.

762
00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:53.182
Chuck Berry.

763
00:52:53.182 --> 00:52:54.364
Lena Horne.

764
00:52:54.364 --> 00:52:56.727
That type of music.

765
00:52:56.727 --> 00:52:57.318
Yeah.

766
00:52:57.318 --> 00:53:03.227
<v ARSENEAULT>Does it bring back memories when you hear it now?

767
00:53:03.227 --> 00:53:03.818
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

768
00:53:03.818 --> 00:53:07.955
I still have some thirty-three an thirds.

769
00:53:07.955 --> 00:53:10.318
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that a record?

770
00:53:10.318 --> 00:53:10.909
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

771
00:53:10.909 --> 00:53:12.091
Uh huh.

772
00:53:12.091 --> 00:53:13.864
Old, old records.

773
00:53:13.864 --> 00:53:14.455
Yeah.

774
00:53:14.455 --> 00:53:16.227
Still have some.

775
00:53:16.227 --> 00:53:22.727
I had some of the, this belonged to my—it’s over there.

776
00:53:22.727 --> 00:53:26.273
That piece of furniture over there?

777
00:53:26.273 --> 00:53:27.455
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

778
00:53:27.455 --> 00:53:31.000
<v PAYNE>That belonged to my great-grandparents.

779
00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:31.690
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s beautiful.

780
00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:32.036
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

781
00:53:32.036 --> 00:53:33.071
That was theirs.

782
00:53:33.071 --> 00:53:35.488
And it’s an old phonograph that you—

783
00:53:35.488 --> 00:53:36.179
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, cool!

784
00:53:36.179 --> 00:53:36.869
<v PAYNE>Yes, yes.

785
00:53:36.869 --> 00:53:39.631
They had some old vinyl records in there.

786
00:53:39.631 --> 00:53:43.429
But I don't know if they’re still in there or not.

787
00:53:43.429 --> 00:53:46.881
And that little stand when we came in the door?

788
00:53:46.881 --> 00:53:48.952
That belonged to my great-grandparents, also.

789
00:53:48.952 --> 00:53:52.060
That was a washstand that was in their house.

790
00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:52.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wow.

791
00:53:52.750 --> 00:53:55.857
<v PAYNE>So I get to keep a bit of them.

792
00:53:55.857 --> 00:53:57.583
Not just in my heart.

793
00:53:57.583 --> 00:53:58.964
<v ARSENEAULT>For history and everything.

794
00:53:58.964 --> 00:54:00.000
<v PAYNE>Mm hmm.

795
00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:03.944
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the Paradise Club?

796
00:54:03.944 --> 00:54:04.930
<v PAYNE>[laughs] Loud.

797
00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:10.352
Rowdy. [laughs] And quite a few of my generation attended there.

798
00:54:10.352 --> 00:54:11.831
Quite a few.

799
00:54:11.831 --> 00:54:20.211
I didn’t go to the Paradise very much, but I did go on a couple of occasions.

800
00:54:20.211 --> 00:54:20.704
Yeah.

801
00:54:20.704 --> 00:54:25.634
Ran by Valery Montgomery, and it was a hangout spot.

802
00:54:25.634 --> 00:54:26.620
<v ARSENEAULT>For adults.

803
00:54:26.620 --> 00:54:27.606
<v PAYNE>For adults.

804
00:54:27.606 --> 00:54:28.592
Mm hmm.

805
00:54:28.592 --> 00:54:30.070
Not for children.

806
00:54:30.070 --> 00:54:31.056
For adults.

807
00:54:31.056 --> 00:54:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How old did you have to be?

808
00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:42.595
<v PAYNE>I would think probably at that time about nineteen, twenty, in order to get in.

809
00:54:42.595 --> 00:54:50.190
Of course, I’m sure Butch slipped in long before he was—[laughs] Butch and my husband.

810
00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:51.203
I’m sure.

811
00:54:51.203 --> 00:54:52.722
<v ARSENEAULT>Who went there?

812
00:54:52.722 --> 00:54:53.228
Just—

813
00:54:53.228 --> 00:54:55.759
<v PAYNE>Local people from around here.

814
00:54:55.759 --> 00:55:00.823
Like I said, Thomas Porter, Butch, Archie Pryor, Charles Porter.

815
00:55:00.823 --> 00:55:02.342
Charles is deceased.

816
00:55:02.342 --> 00:55:02.848
Hmm.

817
00:55:02.848 --> 00:55:10.949
I’m trying to think of someone that was maybe a little bit older than our generation.

818
00:55:10.949 --> 00:55:12.468
I don't know.

819
00:55:12.468 --> 00:55:17.025
My Aunt Tommy might have gone by there, too.

820
00:55:17.025 --> 00:55:24.620
I can’t swear by it, so I won’t say that she did if she didn’t.

821
00:55:24.620 --> 00:55:26.139
Quite a few.

822
00:55:26.139 --> 00:55:34.747
Those that didn’t have a strong belief in God was probably the only ones who didn’t go.

823
00:55:34.747 --> 00:55:40.823
Some of them who had a belief in God, they probably went.

824
00:55:40.823 --> 00:55:42.848
But just, you know.

825
00:55:42.848 --> 00:55:50.949
I can’t swear to it, but probably Coach Williams probably went one time, also. [laughs] Yeah.

826
00:55:50.949 --> 00:55:55.000
And a couple of the teachers, probably.

827
00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:57.237
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of entertainment was there?

828
00:55:57.237 --> 00:56:04.692
<v PAYNE>We had a TV set that had rabbit ears that you could probably only get about three or four channels.

829
00:56:04.692 --> 00:56:09.537
And the Pinky Lee show was one of the ones that I remember.

830
00:56:09.537 --> 00:56:11.774
And that was basically the entertainment.

831
00:56:11.774 --> 00:56:14.383
Other than when we had family gathers.

832
00:56:14.383 --> 00:56:21.838
And we had to be in bed by 8:30 or 9 o’clock, so we couldn’t stay up there very late.

833
00:56:21.838 --> 00:56:24.820
But I remember Pinky Lee and Howdy Doody.

834
00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:26.311
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that a cartoon?

835
00:56:26.311 --> 00:56:28.920
<v PAYNE>Howdy Doody was more like a clown.

836
00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:31.530
Pinky Lee was dressed like a clown.

837
00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:34.139
But they came on in the afternoons.

838
00:56:34.139 --> 00:56:37.494
And that’s what we had a chance to watch.

839
00:56:37.494 --> 00:56:41.221
<v ARSENEAULT>So when that wasn’t on, what was on the TV?

840
00:56:41.221 --> 00:56:41.967
Anything else?

841
00:56:41.967 --> 00:56:44.949
<v PAYNE>The news, and a few little local stories.

842
00:56:44.949 --> 00:56:45.321
Westerns.

843
00:56:45.321 --> 00:56:46.812
Old, old, old westerns.

844
00:56:46.812 --> 00:56:48.676
And that was basically it.

845
00:56:48.676 --> 00:56:49.049
Yeah.

846
00:56:49.049 --> 00:56:56.504
And see, most of the time, like if it was school time, we were going to school during the day.

847
00:56:56.504 --> 00:56:59.486
Come home, have to do your homework first.

848
00:56:59.486 --> 00:57:07.314
Then possibly eat your supper, and we didn’t eat like my kids and my grandkids eat, in front of the TV.

849
00:57:07.314 --> 00:57:11.787
You sit at the table and you ate as a family together.

850
00:57:11.787 --> 00:57:14.769
And then after that, you had your bath.

851
00:57:14.769 --> 00:57:16.632
Then you could watch TV.

852
00:57:16.632 --> 00:57:20.733
So you didn’t get to watch a whole lot of TV.

853
00:57:20.733 --> 00:57:27.069
Now on weekends, you could probably watch on a Saturday morning or something or other like that.

854
00:57:27.069 --> 00:57:29.679
But we had one with rabbit ears.

855
00:57:29.679 --> 00:57:32.661
You’d have to keep adjusting the rabbit ears.

856
00:57:32.661 --> 00:57:36.015
And then they had another antenna that would go

857
00:57:36.015 --> 00:57:37.506
<c.p>up from the outside.

858
00:57:37.506 --> 00:57:43.843
And sometimes you’d have to go and turn the pole if it wasn’t coming in right. [laughs]

859
00:57:43.843 --> 00:57:45.334
<v ARSENEAULT>Stand on your head?

860
00:57:45.334 --> 00:57:45.707
<v PAYNE>Yes.

861
00:57:45.707 --> 00:57:47.571
So that was basically it.

862
00:57:47.571 --> 00:57:52.789
Between the TV set and the going to one of the canteens and whatever.

863
00:57:52.789 --> 00:57:54.653
You had chores to do.

864
00:57:54.653 --> 00:57:57.635
We didn’t have a lot of free time.

865
00:57:57.635 --> 00:58:03.226
As a teenager, I was responsible for washing and ironing my own clothes for school.

866
00:58:03.226 --> 00:58:06.208
So on Saturday mornings, that was my job.

867
00:58:06.208 --> 00:58:11.427
And then, like I said, when school was out, I babysit for my cousins.

868
00:58:11.427 --> 00:58:14.036
So that was most of my day.

869
00:58:14.036 --> 00:58:19.254
I just had some free time in the afternoon, after my parents came home.

870
00:58:19.254 --> 00:58:20.000
See?

871
00:58:20.000 --> 00:58:21.761
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there gendered chores?

872
00:58:21.761 --> 00:58:27.042
Like boys did this kind of work and girls did this kind?

873
00:58:27.042 --> 00:58:27.923
<v PAYNE>Mm hmm.

874
00:58:27.923 --> 00:58:33.204
Boys was more responsible for like cutting the grass and whatever else.

875
00:58:33.204 --> 00:58:36.285
And girls mainly the housework and whatever.

876
00:58:36.285 --> 00:58:47.729
But we were also taught that whether you were a boy or a girl, you need to know how to cook to prepare food for yourself.

877
00:58:47.729 --> 00:58:53.891
So that’s why a lot of males in our family knew how to cook.

878
00:58:53.891 --> 00:58:54.331
Yeah.

879
00:58:54.331 --> 00:58:56.532
So that they could survive.

880
00:58:56.532 --> 00:58:56.972
Yup.

881
00:58:56.972 --> 00:59:05.335
It was basically unheard of in the ‘50s for a woman to go out and cut grass or whatever.

882
00:59:05.335 --> 00:59:07.535
That was a man’s job.

883
00:59:07.535 --> 00:59:13.697
But as women’s lib came along, we found out we had more responsibilities. [laughs]

884
00:59:13.697 --> 00:59:14.577
<v ARSENEAULT>I know.

885
00:59:14.577 --> 00:59:15.018
Hmm.

886
00:59:15.018 --> 00:59:18.539
Not sure if that was good or not.

887
00:59:18.539 --> 00:59:21.180
<v PAYNE>Some of them weren’t so good.

888
00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:25.141
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your family grow plants for like home remedies?

889
00:59:25.141 --> 00:59:26.461
<v PAYNE>No. Just vegetables.

890
00:59:26.461 --> 00:59:27.782
Just for eating.

891
00:59:27.782 --> 00:59:31.303
There was an old lady that delivered babies.

892
00:59:31.303 --> 00:59:35.264
And I think she probably had some home remedies.

893
00:59:35.264 --> 00:59:37.465
Everybody called her Aunt Pinky.

894
00:59:37.465 --> 00:59:41.426
But other than that—oh, and there was another man.

895
00:59:41.426 --> 00:59:44.947
He lived, I want to say up Evergreen.

896
00:59:44.947 --> 00:59:51.549
No. He lived in Sulphur but he came over here lots. [He was a Geary?].

897
00:59:51.549 --> 00:59:54.190
They called him the root man.

898
00:59:54.190 --> 00:59:59.912
He would make all kinds of different teas and different things like that.

899
00:59:59.912 --> 01:00:05.634
But no, basically they didn’t believe in too many home remedies or whatever.

900
01:00:05.634 --> 01:00:12.236
In I would say probably the early ‘50s, Dr. Charles Fellows came to this area.

901
01:00:12.236 --> 01:00:17.077
And he took care of just about everybody in this area.

902
01:00:17.077 --> 01:00:18.838
His office was integrated.

903
01:00:18.838 --> 01:00:21.919
The front of it was for whites.

904
01:00:21.919 --> 01:00:25.000
Go to the back for blacks.

905
01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:26.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he white?

906
01:00:26.286 --> 01:00:27.571
<v PAYNE>He was white.

907
01:00:27.571 --> 01:00:31.429
And probably, it would have been in the ‘50s.

908
01:00:31.429 --> 01:00:34.429
Dad had a problem with [gastric] ulcers.

909
01:00:34.429 --> 01:00:40.000
He had been in the military or whatever and he came back home.

910
01:00:40.000 --> 01:00:47.286
I remember on one particular occasion we were still staying in our [Leo] Prater and them house.

911
01:00:47.286 --> 01:00:54.143
And Mom was trying to get him to the car to get him to the doctor.

912
01:00:54.143 --> 01:01:01.429
And he was just doubled over in the front yard with the pain that was so severe.

913
01:01:01.429 --> 01:01:04.429
And Dr. Fellows did surgery on him.

914
01:01:04.429 --> 01:01:08.714
And he hadn’t had problems with ulcers anymore since then.

915
01:01:08.714 --> 01:01:10.857
Eat anything that he wants.

916
01:01:10.857 --> 01:01:11.714
<v ARSENEAULT>Bacon and—

917
01:01:11.714 --> 01:01:12.571
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

918
01:01:12.571 --> 01:01:14.286
All kinds of stuff.

919
01:01:14.286 --> 01:01:17.714
<v ARSENEAULT>So where did you go for medical treatment?

920
01:01:17.714 --> 01:01:18.571
Besides the—

921
01:01:18.571 --> 01:01:20.286
<v PAYNE>Basically that was it.

922
01:01:20.286 --> 01:01:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That was it?

923
01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:22.389
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

924
01:01:22.389 --> 01:01:25.500
I never had to, I was never sick.

925
01:01:25.500 --> 01:01:29.000
I didn’t even realize that I had chicken pox.

926
01:01:29.000 --> 01:01:34.444
Because if you have chicken pox, then you’re susceptible for the shingle virus, also.

927
01:01:34.444 --> 01:01:36.000
And Mom has shingles.

928
01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:41.056
And one day I was [. . . ?] I said, “Well, I never had chicken pox.”

929
01:01:41.056 --> 01:01:43.000
She said, “Yes, you did.”

930
01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:45.333
I said, “Well, I don't remember.”

931
01:01:45.333 --> 01:01:48.056
She said, “Go look on your forehead.”

932
01:01:48.056 --> 01:01:50.000
She said, “About your nose.

933
01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:53.111
And you’re going to see a little spot.”

934
01:01:53.111 --> 01:01:56.222
And I went and looked and it was.

935
01:01:56.222 --> 01:01:58.944
And she said, “Yes, you’ve had shingles.”

936
01:01:58.944 --> 01:02:05.556
So for colds, running a fever, things like that, we took care of those things at home.

937
01:02:05.556 --> 01:02:05.944
Quinine.

938
01:02:05.944 --> 01:02:11.000
I think it was called Hadacol cough syrup or something another like that.

939
01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:12.944
And that’s what was used.

940
01:02:12.944 --> 01:02:14.500
It was more remedies.

941
01:02:14.500 --> 01:02:20.722
My grandparents and them used the almanac a lot for planning and different things like that.

942
01:02:20.722 --> 01:02:24.222
And they had an old catalog, Watkins something another.

943
01:02:24.222 --> 01:02:28.500
And they would order stuff out of that for medicinal purposes.

944
01:02:28.500 --> 01:02:33.556
So it had to be real, real bad to go to a doctor.

945
01:02:33.556 --> 01:02:39.000
But I was basically healthy, so I didn’t have any of those problems.

946
01:02:39.000 --> 01:02:40.529
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the community?

947
01:02:40.529 --> 01:02:47.407
<v PAYNE>Basically it was probably about the same for all, because the social economic status was basically the same.

948
01:02:47.407 --> 01:02:53.521
The only time that things begin to change, probably toward the end of the late ‘50s,

949
01:02:53.521 --> 01:03:01.546
when some of the fellows was able to get on at the local plants as laborers and different things like that.

950
01:03:01.546 --> 01:03:04.221
Then the income status started coming up.

951
01:03:04.221 --> 01:03:10.718
Dad worked for, Maplewood had a plant that is right behind First Baptist Church back there then.

952
01:03:10.718 --> 01:03:14.157
And they took care of all the sewer system.

953
01:03:14.157 --> 01:03:23.711
And Mr. [George] Braxton [Sr], they just tore his house down, that lived right across from the little church that’s over there on East Burton,

954
01:03:23.711 --> 01:03:31.736
he had a big old truck with a bed on it that had, I guess it was wooden rails or something.

955
01:03:31.736 --> 01:03:37.086
But you could see through it if was nothing on the bed of it.

956
01:03:37.086 --> 01:03:40.907
And he would go pick up all of Maplewood’s trash.

957
01:03:40.907 --> 01:03:46.639
And he would bring it up here to the corner, go down Evergreen [and dump].

958
01:03:46.639 --> 01:03:59.250
And he had a big old pile that he would do some burning and whatever else from that and whatever [and some of the less fortunate would comb through looking for useable items].

959
01:03:59.250 --> 01:04:06.511
But we never were, basically, that sick, any of us that we had to have a lot of attention.

960
01:04:06.511 --> 01:04:14.536
We had a few little broken bones and stuff like that, that you have to go have those set and whatever.

961
01:04:14.536 --> 01:04:21.032
But I don’t ever remember anybody being that seriously ill like scarlet fever or anything of that,

962
01:04:21.032 --> 01:04:26.000
that we had to have any long-term care or anything for that.

963
01:04:26.000 --> 01:04:27.786
<v ARSENEAULT>What about delivering babies?

964
01:04:27.786 --> 01:04:31.357
<v PAYNE>Most of the babies was delivered by midwives.

965
01:04:31.357 --> 01:04:40.286
Now Della says that she was born, now Della’s about three years younger than—no, Della should be younger than that.

966
01:04:40.286 --> 01:04:43.857
<c.p>Della’s about six years younger than I am.

967
01:04:43.857 --> 01:04:49.214
And Della says that she was delivered at home by Dr. Ross.

968
01:04:49.214 --> 01:04:51.893
He came out to deliver her.

969
01:04:51.893 --> 01:04:58.143
Most of the other babies in my time and whatever were delivered by midwives.

970
01:04:58.143 --> 01:05:01.268
And it was one called Aunt Adaline.

971
01:05:01.268 --> 01:05:10.196
And I think the one they call Ms. Pinky, I think those were the two that did all the delivering.

972
01:05:10.196 --> 01:05:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they just community members who knew how to deliver a baby?

973
01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:16.702
<v PAYNE>Mm hmm.

974
01:05:16.702 --> 01:05:20.213
And my birth certificate, she’s messed up my name royally.

975
01:05:20.213 --> 01:05:25.479
My name on my birth certificate is spelled V-e-r-y-, very. [laughs] So I changed it.

976
01:05:25.479 --> 01:05:26.181
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

977
01:05:26.181 --> 01:05:26.532
<v PAYNE>Yes.

978
01:05:26.532 --> 01:05:28.287
<v ARSENEAULT>So they did that, too.

979
01:05:28.287 --> 01:05:30.394
I didn’t know they filled out—

980
01:05:30.394 --> 01:05:31.096
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

981
01:05:31.096 --> 01:05:31.447
Yeah.

982
01:05:31.447 --> 01:05:33.202
They fill out the paper.

983
01:05:33.202 --> 01:05:38.468
Most of them had some degree where they could read and write a little bit.

984
01:05:38.468 --> 01:05:41.979
But some of them couldn’t read and write very much.

985
01:05:41.979 --> 01:05:45.138
That’s how it’s spelled on my original birth certificate.

986
01:05:45.138 --> 01:05:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So she was one of the ones, maybe, that couldn’t?

987
01:05:49.000 --> 01:05:49.366
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

988
01:05:49.366 --> 01:05:54.855
I guess so, because she didn’t spell my name right at all, I’ll tell you.

989
01:05:54.855 --> 01:05:57.417
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did they do?

990
01:05:57.417 --> 01:05:59.613
They just came in the house—

991
01:05:59.613 --> 01:06:00.345
<v PAYNE>Mm hmm.

992
01:06:00.345 --> 01:06:08.030
Came in the house and asked for some warm water and a couple of towels and whatever, and closed the door.

993
01:06:08.030 --> 01:06:12.055
So the next thing you knew, you heard a baby crying.

994
01:06:12.055 --> 01:06:13.153
<v ARSENEAULT>So different now.

995
01:06:13.153 --> 01:06:13.885
<v PAYNE>Mm hmm.

996
01:06:13.885 --> 01:06:14.617
So different.

997
01:06:14.617 --> 01:06:15.349
So different.

998
01:06:15.349 --> 01:06:16.813
Well, in a sense,

999
01:06:16.813 --> 01:06:27.791
yes and no. Because then we went to a period of where the family was kind of pushed away and you went into the delivery room and delivered your baby.

1000
01:06:27.791 --> 01:06:34.013
And now your family can go into the area and be there when the child is born.

1001
01:06:34.013 --> 01:06:39.868
So I guess we kind of mixed a little of both of them in today’s medicine.

1002
01:06:39.868 --> 01:06:44.260
But no. when that door was closed, you know not to go.

1003
01:06:44.260 --> 01:06:53.043
And that was another thing, when adults was talking or visiting with another, children were not to sit around and listen or whatever else.

1004
01:06:53.043 --> 01:06:55.238
You went out and you played.

1005
01:06:55.238 --> 01:06:59.630
Whether it was with a syrup can, these round Steen syrup cans.

1006
01:06:59.630 --> 01:07:07.315
With a stick and you put a nail through it and you made you a little thing that you could push,

1007
01:07:07.315 --> 01:07:10.243
because a can would roll over and over.

1008
01:07:10.243 --> 01:07:11.340
You did that.

1009
01:07:11.340 --> 01:07:12.438
You jumped rope.

1010
01:07:12.438 --> 01:07:13.536
You played jacks.

1011
01:07:13.536 --> 01:07:15.000
Things like that.

1012
01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:20.344
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you know you weren’t supposed to listen?

1013
01:07:20.344 --> 01:07:22.125
<v PAYNE>You were told.

1014
01:07:22.125 --> 01:07:22.719
Yep.

1015
01:07:22.719 --> 01:07:25.094
“Grown folks are talking.

1016
01:07:25.094 --> 01:07:26.875
You go play.”

1017
01:07:26.875 --> 01:07:28.656
Just that simple.

1018
01:07:28.656 --> 01:07:29.250
Yeah.

1019
01:07:29.250 --> 01:07:29.844
See.

1020
01:07:29.844 --> 01:07:31.031
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

1021
01:07:31.031 --> 01:07:32.812
I like that.

1022
01:07:32.812 --> 01:07:34.000
<v PAYNE>Yep.

1023
01:07:34.000 --> 01:07:34.225
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1024
01:07:34.225 --> 01:07:34.675
Let’s see.

1025
01:07:34.675 --> 01:07:35.800
It’s at an hour more.

1026
01:07:35.800 --> 01:07:37.825
Did you want to go for a few more?

1027
01:07:37.825 --> 01:07:38.725
Are you getting tired?

1028
01:07:38.725 --> 01:07:39.400
<v PAYNE>No. I’m fine.

1029
01:07:39.400 --> 01:07:39.850
<v ARSENEAULT>You’re good?

1030
01:07:39.850 --> 01:07:40.525
<v PAYNE>Yeah, I’m good.

1031
01:07:40.525 --> 01:07:41.650
<v ARSENEAULT>You would tell me, right?

1032
01:07:41.650 --> 01:07:42.100
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

1033
01:07:42.100 --> 01:07:42.325
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1034
01:07:42.325 --> 01:07:43.000
<v PAYNE>I’m good.

1035
01:07:43.000 --> 01:07:46.838
<v ARSENEAULT>We talked about where you went to school.

1036
01:07:46.838 --> 01:07:51.155
What kinds of things did you learn in school?

1037
01:07:51.155 --> 01:07:53.554
What were they teaching you?

1038
01:07:53.554 --> 01:07:55.952
<v PAYNE>Ava Prater was Dad’s aunt.

1039
01:07:55.952 --> 01:07:59.310
She was the home ec [economics] teacher.

1040
01:07:59.310 --> 01:08:02.668
She was a master at making things.

1041
01:08:02.668 --> 01:08:03.148
Sewing.

1042
01:08:03.148 --> 01:08:08.424
And so therefore, she expected you to do your very best.

1043
01:08:08.424 --> 01:08:14.181
If there was a seam, she expected that seam to be right.

1044
01:08:14.181 --> 01:08:19.937
If it was a hem, she expected that hem to be right.

1045
01:08:19.937 --> 01:08:21.856
Evelyn West taught math.

1046
01:08:21.856 --> 01:08:28.092
And she had to have loved math, because it just flowed from her.

1047
01:08:28.092 --> 01:08:33.849
And that was a subject I hated. [laughs] But she taught math.

1048
01:08:33.849 --> 01:08:42.004
And Mrs. Spikes taught chemistry and—we didn’t even drink our drinks – Mrs. Spikes taught chemistry and biology.

1049
01:08:42.004 --> 01:08:43.923
This yours, that yours.

1050
01:08:43.923 --> 01:08:48.240
She passed away about two or three weeks ago.

1051
01:08:48.240 --> 01:08:53.517
And I’m thinking someone probably gave you a picture of her.

1052
01:08:53.517 --> 01:09:00.712
There was a lady, Marian Cole, she was an elementary teacher, but she did chorus.

1053
01:09:00.712 --> 01:09:08.867
And she and Ray Mollo worked with us as choir members for the school [Mossville High School].

1054
01:09:08.867 --> 01:09:13.664
A little short lady named Paule was our P.E. coach.

1055
01:09:13.664 --> 01:09:17.982
And P.E. was not like kids take P.E. today.

1056
01:09:17.982 --> 01:09:19.421
You took P.E.

1057
01:09:19.421 --> 01:09:22.779
You exercised for X amount of minutes.

1058
01:09:22.779 --> 01:09:28.535
And then if it was basketball season, you did a basketball game.

1059
01:09:28.535 --> 01:09:40.528
Or if it was something with the softball and the weather was fine, you went out and you got on the lawn behind the school.

1060
01:09:40.528 --> 01:09:42.926
And you participated in that.

1061
01:09:42.926 --> 01:09:45.325
And we were very active.

1062
01:09:45.325 --> 01:09:52.041
If I remember correctly, I only had about twenty-two people – that was Ms. Spikes.

1063
01:09:52.041 --> 01:09:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1064
01:09:53.000 --> 01:09:57.801
<v PAYNE>I only had about twenty-two people that graduated in my class.

1065
01:09:57.801 --> 01:10:03.912
And out of the twenty-two, we had one that you could say was obese.

1066
01:10:03.912 --> 01:10:06.531
And then we had one that

1067
01:10:06.531 --> 01:10:09.586
<c.p>you could say was a little heavy.

1068
01:10:09.586 --> 01:10:12.642
It’s because kids got out and played.

1069
01:10:12.642 --> 01:10:21.371
They burned up all of that energy instead of sitting in front of a screen pushing buttons and whatever else.

1070
01:10:21.371 --> 01:10:22.681
I enjoyed it.

1071
01:10:22.681 --> 01:10:25.300
And I think they liked teaching.

1072
01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:28.792
Ms. Mitchell was one of my elementary teachers.

1073
01:10:28.792 --> 01:10:34.902
And she probably was, she wasn’t that old, but she probably was developing Parkinson’s.

1074
01:10:34.902 --> 01:10:42.759
Because I can remember a little twitch in her jaw when she would be talking or whatever else.

1075
01:10:42.759 --> 01:10:44.068
But they taught.

1076
01:10:44.068 --> 01:10:48.433
When we stepped in those classrooms, it was no play.

1077
01:10:48.433 --> 01:10:55.417
They were there to teach us, and if we were willing to learn, they were there.

1078
01:10:55.417 --> 01:10:59.782
And later on, she didn’t teach me. [coughs] Excuse me.

1079
01:10:59.782 --> 01:11:00.655
Amy Reynolds.

1080
01:11:00.655 --> 01:11:06.765
That’s one of the people that I really feel like you should talk to.

1081
01:11:06.765 --> 01:11:09.821
I’m told she lives in Moss Bluff.

1082
01:11:09.821 --> 01:11:12.876
Amy taught my daughter and her generation.

1083
01:11:12.876 --> 01:11:13.749
Amy’s white.

1084
01:11:13.749 --> 01:11:19.423
Amy made those kids feel like they were a part of her family.

1085
01:11:19.423 --> 01:11:26.844
And I feel like they knew where the line was not to cross it in reference to,

1086
01:11:26.844 --> 01:11:34.700
not to push it for discipline or whatever else, or to take advantage of it or whatever else.

1087
01:11:34.700 --> 01:11:39.502
But all of them just seemed like they loved Amy Reynolds.

1088
01:11:39.502 --> 01:11:42.557
And we just had some no-nonsense teachers.

1089
01:11:42.557 --> 01:11:48.668
Probably some of them were a little partying afterwards, because they were all young.

1090
01:11:48.668 --> 01:11:52.160
But we didn’t know that in the classroom.

1091
01:11:52.160 --> 01:11:59.580
Now maybe Butch or one or two of the others, the fellows who kind of ran around.

1092
01:11:59.580 --> 01:12:04.381
But most of the girls, we didn’t even know those things.

1093
01:12:04.381 --> 01:12:07.000
So we respected our teachers.

1094
01:12:07.000 --> 01:12:10.173
<v ARSENEAULT>How were your parents involved in your schooling?

1095
01:12:10.173 --> 01:12:13.742
<v PAYNE>PTA, well, it was PTA at the time, meetings.

1096
01:12:13.742 --> 01:12:22.071
And they had those basically about every, I think it was every six weeks, I believe, there was a PTA meeting.

1097
01:12:22.071 --> 01:12:27.624
And when it was report card time, they wanted to see that report card.

1098
01:12:27.624 --> 01:12:31.193
They made sure that I had time available in

1099
01:12:31.193 --> 01:12:33.573
<c.p>the afternoons to do my homework.

1100
01:12:33.573 --> 01:12:43.488
And if I needed any assistance they couldn’t help me, because neither one of them had high school educations, but they found help for me.

1101
01:12:43.488 --> 01:12:43.885
Resources.

1102
01:12:43.885 --> 01:12:48.247
And when it came time for me to go to college,

1103
01:12:48.247 --> 01:12:58.163
how they were going to come up with the money for me to go to college, I don't know, but they came up with it.

1104
01:12:58.163 --> 01:13:03.715
And I was so in love with Wilford that I flunked my first semester.

1105
01:13:03.715 --> 01:13:13.234
So then after that, I had to pay for college out of my own pocket. [laughs] We had to go to school in Lafayette.

1106
01:13:13.234 --> 01:13:15.614
Well, Grambling and Southern was there.

1107
01:13:15.614 --> 01:13:24.339
But one of my classmates and I, we wanted to go to nursing school so we were going to go to Lafayette.

1108
01:13:24.339 --> 01:13:26.719
We want to go to school.

1109
01:13:26.719 --> 01:13:29.892
We couldn’t stay on campus because of segregation.

1110
01:13:29.892 --> 01:13:33.858
So we stayed at a lady’s house on Johnson [Street].

1111
01:13:33.858 --> 01:13:37.031
And it was just a couple of blocks.

1112
01:13:37.031 --> 01:13:40.997
And we had to walk back and forth to school.

1113
01:13:40.997 --> 01:13:46.946
And Wilford and her boyfriend would come down if not every weekend, every other weekend.

1114
01:13:46.946 --> 01:13:50.515
And I was just too in love to study.

1115
01:13:50.515 --> 01:13:54.878
I didn't realize the importance of an education at that time.

1116
01:13:54.878 --> 01:13:57.258
I was too busy having fun.

1117
01:13:57.258 --> 01:14:01.224
Got smacked with reality. [laughs] And I don’t regret it.

1118
01:14:01.224 --> 01:14:03.207
It was a learning experience.

1119
01:14:03.207 --> 01:14:04.000
Yeah.

1120
01:14:04.000 --> 01:14:08.698
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you know you were in love with Wilford?

1121
01:14:08.698 --> 01:14:15.276
<v PAYNE>I didn’t tell you the whole story about Mom picking me up that night.

1122
01:14:15.276 --> 01:14:17.155
And it was innocent.

1123
01:14:17.155 --> 01:14:18.565
It was innocent.

1124
01:14:18.565 --> 01:14:26.082
Thomas Porter and them lived at that time on an end of the dead end street.

1125
01:14:26.082 --> 01:14:26.552
Okay?

1126
01:14:26.552 --> 01:14:32.659
And like I said, there wasn’t that many houses over there in Queensboro.

1127
01:14:32.659 --> 01:14:37.828
You could count them on probably one and a half hands.

1128
01:14:37.828 --> 01:14:46.754
But just past their house, they had apparently graded a space, I guess, maybe 150, 200 foot, [dirt road?].

1129
01:14:46.754 --> 01:14:52.392
And it was kind of dirt and gravel and all mixed together.

1130
01:14:52.392 --> 01:14:57.560
It was not like a true road or anything like that.

1131
01:14:57.560 --> 01:15:01.319
<c.p>So this would have been probably around 8:30ish.

1132
01:15:01.319 --> 01:15:02.728
Early evening now.

1133
01:15:02.728 --> 01:15:04.138
But it’s dark.

1134
01:15:04.138 --> 01:15:11.655
Wilford and I, Irie and Poky, Lavonia and Clarence, it was about six or seven couples.

1135
01:15:11.655 --> 01:15:16.823
We had just walked down to the end of that [extension].

1136
01:15:16.823 --> 01:15:26.220
And each one was in a little different spot or whatever else, and we were just doing a little necking.

1137
01:15:26.220 --> 01:15:29.039
It was standing up, you know.

1138
01:15:29.039 --> 01:15:32.797
Who drives up but Irene, Alean, and Dora.

1139
01:15:32.797 --> 01:15:37.966
And I caught the worst whipping of my life that night.

1140
01:15:37.966 --> 01:15:41.254
And I really hadn’t done anything wrong.

1141
01:15:41.254 --> 01:15:51.591
And I told her later when I became an adult, “You whipped me because I embarrassed you in front of your family.”

1142
01:15:51.591 --> 01:15:53.000
Well. [laughs]

1143
01:15:53.000 --> 01:15:54.619
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s your point? [laughter]

1144
01:15:54.619 --> 01:15:55.429
<v PAYNE>I know.

1145
01:15:55.429 --> 01:15:55.833
Yeah.

1146
01:15:55.833 --> 01:15:58.262
I wasn’t really doing anything wrong.

1147
01:15:58.262 --> 01:16:00.286
Just a little simple kissing.

1148
01:16:00.286 --> 01:16:01.905
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were you?

1149
01:16:01.905 --> 01:16:03.929
<v PAYNE>I was about sixteen, seventeen.

1150
01:16:03.929 --> 01:16:05.952
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s when you met Wilford?

1151
01:16:05.952 --> 01:16:06.762
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

1152
01:16:06.762 --> 01:16:09.190
We were in high school together.

1153
01:16:09.190 --> 01:16:15.667
Then believe it or not, after I flunked out, we broke up for about two years.

1154
01:16:15.667 --> 01:16:19.310
And then we went back together [married in 1969].

1155
01:16:19.310 --> 01:16:19.714
Yeah.

1156
01:16:19.714 --> 01:16:20.524
<v ARSENEAULT>True love.

1157
01:16:20.524 --> 01:16:21.333
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

1158
01:16:21.333 --> 01:16:21.738
Yup.

1159
01:16:21.738 --> 01:16:23.762
I enjoyed my childhood here.

1160
01:16:23.762 --> 01:16:27.000
I think this is mine that I—

1161
01:16:27.000 --> 01:16:27.975
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, ma’am.

1162
01:16:27.975 --> 01:16:36.745
I was going to ask if you notice any major differences between your education and your children’s education.

1163
01:16:36.745 --> 01:16:37.233
<v PAYNE>Yes.

1164
01:16:37.233 --> 01:16:49.902
I cannot understand, for the life of me, okay, the education that was taught, and I have no qualms against modern technology and all those things.

1165
01:16:49.902 --> 01:16:55.262
We were taught the basics: reading, writing, arithmetic and [new sciences?].

1166
01:16:55.262 --> 01:17:04.033
Out of those, doctors, lawyers, nurses, teachers, anyone who wanted to become or wanted to learn could succeed.

1167
01:17:04.033 --> 01:17:04.520
Okay?

1168
01:17:04.520 --> 01:17:11.342
Today, we have to have someone write a program to introduce to the children.

1169
01:17:11.342 --> 01:17:14.265
And half of them aren’t learning.

1170
01:17:14.265 --> 01:17:25.960
And we still pay huge amounts of money for these programs that have either failed in another state or is failing in our state.

1171
01:17:25.960 --> 01:17:28.396
And we don’t change them.

1172
01:17:28.396 --> 01:17:34.244
We just keep on doing the same thing over and over again.

1173
01:17:34.244 --> 01:17:39.116
And I think really, and there’s nothing wrong with it.

1174
01:17:39.116 --> 01:17:47.887
If there’s a way that both of them could be integrated where there was more of your basics.

1175
01:17:47.887 --> 01:17:56.171
I have gone to Wal-Mart and there’s people who cannot count that is operating their cash registers.

1176
01:17:56.171 --> 01:18:02.993
And they have one on now, but they have the price marked for it,

1177
01:18:02.993 --> 01:18:10.302
I think it was three twelve-packs of Coke for ten dollars, I think it was.

1178
01:18:10.302 --> 01:18:15.175
And the little girl was trying to figure it up.

1179
01:18:15.175 --> 01:18:23.945
And I said, “It’s $3.37 plus tax,” if you want to be technical point, whatever else it was.

1180
01:18:23.945 --> 01:18:26.869
She could not figure it up!

1181
01:18:26.869 --> 01:18:28.331
They can’t count.

1182
01:18:28.331 --> 01:18:32.716
They’ve grown accustomed, and there’s nothing wrong with calculators.

1183
01:18:32.716 --> 01:18:41.000
But I think you need to know your basics before you go to those particular things.

1184
01:18:41.000 --> 01:18:43.918
<c.p>So I appreciate the education that I got.

1185
01:18:43.918 --> 01:18:50.483
When Gloria Rogers walked in that classroom, we knew that she was going to teach us English, okay?

1186
01:18:50.483 --> 01:18:55.590
And her point was if she found that you wasn’t paying attention or whatever,

1187
01:18:55.590 --> 01:19:00.331
as Omar Khayyam says in The Rubaiyat, the moving finger have moveth on.

1188
01:19:00.331 --> 01:19:06.167
Which meant, you’re sleeping and your eyes are wide open so you better get yourself together.

1189
01:19:06.167 --> 01:19:07.626
Because we’re moving on.

1190
01:19:07.626 --> 01:19:09.450
And Ray Mollo wore glasses.

1191
01:19:09.450 --> 01:19:16.015
I don't think Mr. Mollo, yeah, he must have taught me in seventh grade, I think it was.

1192
01:19:16.015 --> 01:19:22.945
And when you were doing something that was contrary or disturbing his class, those little eyes got beady, okay?

1193
01:19:22.945 --> 01:19:28.781
And it was nothing for him to throw an eraser at you to get your attention.

1194
01:19:28.781 --> 01:19:32.793
And we didn’t take it as abuse or anything like that.

1195
01:19:32.793 --> 01:19:34.617
They were there to teach.

1196
01:19:34.617 --> 01:19:36.805
And they wanted us to learn.

1197
01:19:36.805 --> 01:19:40.818
And that was their way of calling our attention to it.

1198
01:19:40.818 --> 01:19:43.371
Like this one in there [her grandson],

1199
01:19:43.371 --> 01:19:51.760
he told them when he was in the tenth grade to catch a football going I don't know how many hours an hour,

1200
01:19:51.760 --> 01:19:53.948
he was very good at it.

1201
01:19:53.948 --> 01:19:55.772
In typing, he was lazy.

1202
01:19:55.772 --> 01:19:58.325
He didn’t want to correct his mistakes.

1203
01:19:58.325 --> 01:20:01.608
So the typing teacher told him something about it.

1204
01:20:01.608 --> 01:20:04.526
He says, “I’ve got arthritis in my hands.”

1205
01:20:04.526 --> 01:20:14.009
Now if we had pulled that kind of bull with one of our teachers, we would have been sent to the office in a Minnesota minute.

1206
01:20:14.009 --> 01:20:16.562
The principal would have paddled our behind.

1207
01:20:16.562 --> 01:20:20.939
And when we got home, our parents would have paddled our behinds.

1208
01:20:20.939 --> 01:20:21.669
We knew.

1209
01:20:21.669 --> 01:20:23.857
Do what you have to do.

1210
01:20:23.857 --> 01:20:25.316
And that was it.

1211
01:20:25.316 --> 01:20:27.140
You don’t have no problem.

1212
01:20:27.140 --> 01:20:28.964
I wasn’t a bad student.

1213
01:20:28.964 --> 01:20:30.787
I wasn’t the best student.

1214
01:20:30.787 --> 01:20:33.340
I was a C/B student, I’d say.

1215
01:20:33.340 --> 01:20:41.000
And when I went to college, I really had to study in order to make a few As there, whatever.

1216
01:20:41.000 --> 01:20:44.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Which you got your degree in nursing?

1217
01:20:44.500 --> 01:20:45.000
<v PAYNE>Nursing.

1218
01:20:45.000 --> 01:20:46.000
Mm hmm.

1219
01:20:46.000 --> 01:20:54.000
And turned out to be, if I may say myself, a pretty darn good one. [laughter]

1220
01:20:54.000 --> 01:20:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So how were decisions made in your family?

1221
01:20:58.000 --> 01:21:01.000
How did that work, usually?

1222
01:21:01.000 --> 01:21:03.379
<v PAYNE>My parents basically made them together.

1223
01:21:03.379 --> 01:21:08.136
It was no, I say whatever, I say go or whatever else.

1224
01:21:08.136 --> 01:21:13.686
And if one made a decision independently, the other one pretty well supported them.

1225
01:21:13.686 --> 01:21:19.237
Because I think they kind of knew what each other expected of each other.

1226
01:21:19.237 --> 01:21:22.805
And how we were going to be brought up.

1227
01:21:22.805 --> 01:21:25.580
So there was no problem with that.

1228
01:21:25.580 --> 01:21:27.166
I was blessed! [laughs]

1229
01:21:27.166 --> 01:21:33.112
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that how the community was, too, or was it just different for every family?

1230
01:21:33.112 --> 01:21:39.059
<v PAYNE>No, I think it might have been a few exceptions, but basically it was that.

1231
01:21:39.059 --> 01:21:48.970
Dad had a way of saying, “I told you,” and you knew when he pointed that finger he meant business, so you cut it out.

1232
01:21:48.970 --> 01:21:50.556
Dad never whipped me.

1233
01:21:50.556 --> 01:21:51.746
Not at all.

1234
01:21:51.746 --> 01:21:53.728
<v ARSENEAULT>It was always your mom.

1235
01:21:53.728 --> 01:21:56.107
<v PAYNE>Mm hmm. [laughs] Mean old lady.

1236
01:21:56.107 --> 01:21:57.692
No, she’s not. [laughs]

1237
01:21:57.692 --> 01:22:01.260
<v ARSENEAULT>I got whipped, but it was always my dad.

1238
01:22:01.260 --> 01:22:03.243
<v PAYNE>And she’s left hand, too.

1239
01:22:03.243 --> 01:22:06.811
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, so you have to like turn your body?

1240
01:22:06.811 --> 01:22:08.000
<v PAYNE>Uh huh.

1241
01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:12.762
<v ARSENEAULT>So who made decisions like approving of your marriage and that kind of stuff?

1242
01:22:12.762 --> 01:22:15.143
<v PAYNE>I told you, I ran off. [laughs]

1243
01:22:15.143 --> 01:22:17.864
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, so they didn’t even know about it.

1244
01:22:17.864 --> 01:22:22.626
<v PAYNE>That would have been more Mom by me being a girl or whatever else.

1245
01:22:22.626 --> 01:22:25.007
But I decided that I was going.

1246
01:22:25.007 --> 01:22:29.429
So I called a friend of mine, which happened to be my cousin.

1247
01:22:29.429 --> 01:22:36.231
And he got a friend of his and we drove across the state line to Orange and got married there.

1248
01:22:36.231 --> 01:22:40.653
When I came home, “Mom, I’m married!” [laughs] Couldn’t do nothing about it!

1249
01:22:40.653 --> 01:22:42.014
<v ARSENEAULT>What did she say?

1250
01:22:42.014 --> 01:22:48.816
<v PAYNE>Well, by that time, they had gotten to know each other well, so she didn't have any problems with it.

1251
01:22:48.816 --> 01:22:49.837
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s so funny.

1252
01:22:49.837 --> 01:22:51.197
<v PAYNE>But I was twenty.

1253
01:22:51.197 --> 01:22:52.558
I was an adult.

1254
01:22:52.558 --> 01:22:54.599
<v ARSENEAULT>You’re old enough to get married.

1255
01:22:54.599 --> 01:22:54.939
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

1256
01:22:54.939 --> 01:22:58.000
I was old enough to make that decision.

1257
01:22:58.000 --> 01:22:58.992
<v ARSENEAULT>So young!

1258
01:22:58.992 --> 01:23:02.961
You already told me where you attended church.

1259
01:23:02.961 --> 01:23:09.409
So what kind of values do you think your parents instilled in you?

1260
01:23:09.409 --> 01:23:11.890
<v PAYNE>A sense of responsibility, honesty.

1261
01:23:11.890 --> 01:23:15.858
If you make a mistake, you own it.

1262
01:23:15.858 --> 01:23:21.315
Strong belief in God, that with him all things are possible.

1263
01:23:21.315 --> 01:23:26.276
And back to mistakes, we’re all human, we make mistakes.

1264
01:23:26.276 --> 01:23:34.709
So it’s no reason to let one mistake bog you down for the rest of your life.

1265
01:23:34.709 --> 01:23:35.701
Work ethics.

1266
01:23:35.701 --> 01:23:37.189
Being on time.

1267
01:23:37.189 --> 01:23:40.661
Priding yourself in doing a good job.

1268
01:23:40.661 --> 01:23:48.598
And in my case, like with nursing, caring about the people that you take care of.

1269
01:23:48.598 --> 01:23:55.047
Not just there to collect a pay check and walk out the door.

1270
01:23:55.047 --> 01:23:58.024
So I was blessed with that.

1271
01:23:58.024 --> 01:24:01.992
And it didn’t just happen with my parents.

1272
01:24:01.992 --> 01:24:04.969
It was her siblings and all.

1273
01:24:04.969 --> 01:24:07.449
They instilled the same thing.

1274
01:24:07.449 --> 01:24:13.402
My grandfather died, like I said, when I was eight or ten.

1275
01:24:13.402 --> 01:24:21.339
We know now that it was probably from heat stroke because it was in the summer.

1276
01:24:21.339 --> 01:24:23.323
He probably got overheated.

1277
01:24:23.323 --> 01:24:31.260
But my aunt who just passed away with Alzheimer’s, she always prided herself on her memory.

1278
01:24:31.260 --> 01:24:33.740
She had a fantastic memory.

1279
01:24:33.740 --> 01:24:43.165
And God still allowed her to have that part of her up until the very last that she died.

1280
01:24:43.165 --> 01:24:47.630
Three weeks before she died, I was at her

1281
01:24:47.630 --> 01:24:48.126
<c.p>bedside.

1282
01:24:48.126 --> 01:24:51.102
And I got ready to leave.

1283
01:24:51.102 --> 01:24:56.559
And I said, “Aunt Alean, is there anything that you need.”

1284
01:24:56.559 --> 01:24:57.055
“Yeah.”

1285
01:24:57.055 --> 01:24:59.039
I said, “What’s that?”

1286
01:24:59.039 --> 01:25:04.000
“Money.” [laughter] And she did focus on money, too.

1287
01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:04.783
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

1288
01:25:04.783 --> 01:25:06.348
<v PAYNE>That woman could save.

1289
01:25:06.348 --> 01:25:09.870
I told you, they ate a lot of beans.

1290
01:25:09.870 --> 01:25:11.435
She was saving money.

1291
01:25:11.435 --> 01:25:12.217
So yeah.

1292
01:25:12.217 --> 01:25:14.565
Just a strong basic value system.

1293
01:25:14.565 --> 01:25:21.217
You know, don’t do anything to nobody else that you wouldn’t want them to do to you.

1294
01:25:21.217 --> 01:25:23.174
And you treat everybody right.

1295
01:25:23.174 --> 01:25:27.478
And even if someone does you an injustice or whatever else.

1296
01:25:27.478 --> 01:25:31.783
And if you can’t sit down and rectify it, move on.

1297
01:25:31.783 --> 01:25:36.870
God will deal with it in his own time and his own way.

1298
01:25:36.870 --> 01:25:38.826
So I appreciate those values.

1299
01:25:38.826 --> 01:25:47.043
And I don't think there’s a place that I have worked that if I wanted to go back that I couldn’t.

1300
01:25:47.043 --> 01:25:50.957
I’ve worked at Kaiser Permanente in California, Hollywood Presbyterian, Cedars-Sinai.

1301
01:25:50.957 --> 01:25:54.870
Those are three of the biggest hospitals in Los Angeles.

1302
01:25:54.870 --> 01:25:56.043
Psychiatric hospital, Edgemont.

1303
01:25:56.043 --> 01:26:01.130
Five years before I left for California, and twenty-seven years after I left,

1304
01:26:01.130 --> 01:26:08.174
came back to Saint Pat’s and I was waiting on a position to come open at Saint Pat’s.

1305
01:26:08.174 --> 01:26:10.913
I worked a year at Cal Cam.

1306
01:26:10.913 --> 01:26:17.957
And Dr. Kent Seal paid me one of the highest compliments that any doctor could give a nurse.

1307
01:26:17.957 --> 01:26:26.957
And when he found out I was leaving, he came to me and he said, “What would it take to keep you here?”

1308
01:26:26.957 --> 01:26:32.043
And I said, “Well, I’ve already gave them my word that I’m coming.

1309
01:26:32.043 --> 01:26:33.609
So I can’t change.”

1310
01:26:33.609 --> 01:26:35.174
And he said, “Okay.”

1311
01:26:35.174 --> 01:26:38.696
He said, “Well, it’s going to be our loss.”

1312
01:26:38.696 --> 01:26:41.826
But to me, that meant an awful lot.

1313
01:26:41.826 --> 01:26:43.000
It does.

1314
01:26:43.000 --> 01:26:48.415
And I’ve retired, and I go back and work at a nursing home, Holly Hill.

1315
01:26:48.415 --> 01:26:52.024
Patients tell me, well, not the patients, their family member.

1316
01:26:52.024 --> 01:26:54.551
And I can cite you three cases.

1317
01:26:54.551 --> 01:26:59.605
How in the world this woman fell out of bed, I do not know.

1318
01:26:59.605 --> 01:27:01.049
She had a stroke.

1319
01:27:01.049 --> 01:27:02.493
She’s paralyzed on one

1320
01:27:02.493 --> 01:27:02.854
<c.p>side.

1321
01:27:02.854 --> 01:27:05.741
She cannot help herself in the least bit.

1322
01:27:05.741 --> 01:27:10.795
I went down the hall like twenty minutes ago and her door was open.

1323
01:27:10.795 --> 01:27:12.239
I looked at her.

1324
01:27:12.239 --> 01:27:14.405
She was laying in the bed.

1325
01:27:14.405 --> 01:27:18.376
And I come back down the hall, pick up some medicine.

1326
01:27:18.376 --> 01:27:26.678
And I’m going to go give her some medicine and I turn to walk in her room and she’s laying on the floor.

1327
01:27:26.678 --> 01:27:29.205
I says, “Bobbie, I can’t believe this.

1328
01:27:29.205 --> 01:27:30.649
How in the world?”

1329
01:27:30.649 --> 01:27:36.424
I got on the phone and I called her family after I assessed her and everything.

1330
01:27:36.424 --> 01:27:37.868
I called her daughter.

1331
01:27:37.868 --> 01:27:42.561
And her daughter asked me, she said, “Vera, do I need to come?”

1332
01:27:42.561 --> 01:27:45.449
And I said, “Only if you want to.”

1333
01:27:45.449 --> 01:27:53.029
She said, “Well, if was anybody else who told me that,” she said, “I would be on my way up there.

1334
01:27:53.029 --> 01:27:57.000
But with you doing it, I feel comfortable with it.”

1335
01:27:57.000 --> 01:28:01.880
And I have had about three or four family members.

1336
01:28:01.880 --> 01:28:05.297
One of them is another little lady.

1337
01:28:05.297 --> 01:28:11.153
And if something goes wrong with her mother, she won’t call me.

1338
01:28:11.153 --> 01:28:23.354
But she’ll tell the nurses, “You call Vera and you ask Vera if it’s okay to do this or if it’s okay to do that.”

1339
01:28:23.354 --> 01:28:29.699
And a part of it makes me feel good because they trust me.

1340
01:28:29.699 --> 01:28:35.067
Because they know that I’m going to look after their family.

1341
01:28:35.067 --> 01:28:38.971
Well, a lot of people do not care.

1342
01:28:38.971 --> 01:28:41.411
They’re there for a dollar.

1343
01:28:41.411 --> 01:28:42.876
And that’s it.

1344
01:28:42.876 --> 01:28:46.292
And that [values] came from my parents.

1345
01:28:46.292 --> 01:28:47.756
Strong work ethics.

1346
01:28:47.756 --> 01:28:49.708
And I appreciate that.

1347
01:28:49.708 --> 01:28:54.589
Mom has worked at Maplewood Nursery on Sundays and Wednesdays.

1348
01:28:54.589 --> 01:29:04.349
And from the time she was probably seventeen through, she didn’t have to, but she did it, a year ago.

1349
01:29:04.349 --> 01:29:06.301
Cleaned other people’s homes.

1350
01:29:06.301 --> 01:29:09.230
And I know, they worked hard.

1351
01:29:09.230 --> 01:29:20.455
Dad probably was bringing home, after he got out of the military, working for Maplewood, maybe forty, fifty dollars a week after tax.

1352
01:29:20.455 --> 01:29:22.895
And we never went without.

1353
01:29:22.895 --> 01:29:26.799
We always had a roof over our heads.

1354
01:29:26.799 --> 01:29:29.239
Lights was always turned on.

1355
01:29:29.239 --> 01:29:32.167
And we had our needs fulfilled.

1356
01:29:32.167 --> 01:29:34.608
And I’m grateful for that.

1357
01:29:34.608 --> 01:29:39.000
I love them a little bit, too. [laughs]

1358
01:29:39.000 --> 01:29:40.312
<v ARSENEAULT>Last question.

1359
01:29:40.312 --> 01:29:52.125
Do you remember any of the stories that elders used to tell you about growing up in Mossville?

1360
01:29:52.125 --> 01:29:52.781
<v PAYNE>Yes.

1361
01:29:52.781 --> 01:30:00.656
I was told repeatedly there was five families that settled in Mossville.

1362
01:30:00.656 --> 01:30:05.906
Two of them I’m not totally sure about.

1363
01:30:05.906 --> 01:30:09.844
The Mosses, the Rigmaidens, the Braxtons.

1364
01:30:09.844 --> 01:30:15.094
And it was either/or with the next three.

1365
01:30:15.094 --> 01:30:19.031
Perkins, Vincents, or the Lyons, okay?

1366
01:30:19.031 --> 01:30:22.969
Now great-great-grandmother Lyons came from Jamaica.

1367
01:30:22.969 --> 01:30:26.250
And she married great-grandfather Stafford.

1368
01:30:26.250 --> 01:30:36.094
And then Grandpa Earthy and Grandma Eunice [Towner] got married somewhere around 1928 or 1929.

1369
01:30:36.094 --> 01:30:47.906
So that tells me that even though their families came from like the Opelousas area or wherever else,

1370
01:30:47.906 --> 01:30:54.469
they were not the total original settlers that were here.

1371
01:30:54.469 --> 01:31:03.000
It was in those first five or six families that was here.

1372
01:31:03.000 --> 01:31:12.625
All these fourteen and fifteen families that they’re talking about settled here came here in the late ‘20s and the early ‘30s.

1373
01:31:12.625 --> 01:31:20.500
I was told that down by, well, the Mount Zion Baptist Church, it was donated by Jim Moss.

1374
01:31:20.500 --> 01:31:24.875
And he had a sugar cane mill and whatever else.

1375
01:31:24.875 --> 01:31:32.312
And he was, at that time, before Dallas Moss and them, one of the main community leaders.

1376
01:31:32.312 --> 01:31:38.438
But in reference to some of the stories that I was told growing up,

1377
01:31:38.438 --> 01:31:47.625
most of them was all related because Mossville was divided into one time into two different areas: Shoats’ Prairie and Saprack.

1378
01:31:47.625 --> 01:31:55.938
And they used to tell all kinds of ghost stories about Saprack. [laughs] So growing up as a child—

1379
01:31:55.938 --> 01:31:56.812
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s haunted?

1380
01:31:56.812 --> 01:31:57.250
<v PAYNE>Yes.

1381
01:31:57.250 --> 01:32:01.188
I heard a lot of ghost stories about Saprack.

1382
01:32:01.188 --> 01:32:01.625
Yeah.

1383
01:32:01.625 --> 01:32:04.250
<v ARSENEAULT>I heard Evergreen Road is haunted.

1384
01:32:04.250 --> 01:32:04.688
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

1385
01:32:04.688 --> 01:32:07.750
I heard a lot of those. [laughs]

1386
01:32:07.750 --> 01:32:09.938
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of them?

1387
01:32:09.938 --> 01:32:14.312
<v PAYNE>There was this old sycamore tree that grew up there.

1388
01:32:14.312 --> 01:32:25.688
And they would tell you that you didn’t want to get caught around those sycamore trees after it got dark at night and stuff like that,

1389
01:32:25.688 --> 01:32:28.312
and different things would come out.

1390
01:32:28.312 --> 01:32:33.125
One of the guys that lived on Evergreen was a Jordan.

1391
01:32:33.125 --> 01:32:34.000
Oscar Jordan.

1392
01:32:34.000 --> 01:32:37.500
And he was nothing but a jokester, okay?

1393
01:32:37.500 --> 01:32:46.688
He was a chef at one of the major restaurants back in the, this would have been ‘60s, ‘50s and ‘60s.

1394
01:32:46.688 --> 01:32:50.188
I don't remember exactly what year he passed.

1395
01:32:50.188 --> 01:32:53.250
He would tell all kinds of stories.

1396
01:32:53.250 --> 01:32:59.375
Anything that popped in his head, he could tell you a story about it.

1397
01:32:59.375 --> 01:33:02.875
He would tell you a story about ghosts.

1398
01:33:02.875 --> 01:33:08.562
And there was one and I’m trying to remember it about a squirrel.

1399
01:33:08.562 --> 01:33:12.062
It was something to do with the telephone.

1400
01:33:12.062 --> 01:33:21.250
But anyway, he said the squirrel was on the telephone pole up there and he was using the telephone or whatever.

1401
01:33:21.250 --> 01:33:25.188
He could dream up all kinds of things, yeah.

1402
01:33:25.188 --> 01:33:30.438
But I heard a lot of ghost stories growing up about Saprack.

1403
01:33:30.438 --> 01:33:37.000
None really comes to mind right now, but that was one of the things.

1404
01:33:37.000 --> 01:33:37.696
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s interesting.

1405
01:33:37.696 --> 01:33:39.783
I wonder why Saprack was haunted.

1406
01:33:39.783 --> 01:33:41.522
<v PAYNE>I have no earthly idea.

1407
01:33:41.522 --> 01:33:42.217
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

1408
01:33:42.217 --> 01:33:42.565
<v PAYNE>None.

1409
01:33:42.565 --> 01:33:47.783
As far as I know, it was not a burial site or anything like that.

1410
01:33:47.783 --> 01:33:51.957
But it was a lot of ghost stories that came from that.

1411
01:33:51.957 --> 01:33:52.652
<v ARSENEAULT>All right.

1412
01:33:52.652 --> 01:33:55.087
Well we’re at an hour and thirty.

1413
01:33:55.087 --> 01:33:55.435
<v PAYNE>Okay.

1414
01:33:55.435 --> 01:33:57.174
<v ARSENEAULT>So we can stop here.

1415
01:33:57.174 --> 01:34:01.000
Is there anything else you wanted to say about Mossville?

1416
01:34:01.000 --> 01:34:02.340
<v PAYNE>No. Well, yes.

1417
01:34:02.340 --> 01:34:04.574
I grew up basically here.

1418
01:34:04.574 --> 01:34:07.702
And I moved away for eight years.

1419
01:34:07.702 --> 01:34:13.511
And I came back here and I’ve been back here about thirty-four years.

1420
01:34:13.511 --> 01:34:17.979
So this has basically been my home all my life.

1421
01:34:17.979 --> 01:34:21.106
And I hate to see Mossville leave.

1422
01:34:21.106 --> 01:34:22.000
It’s gone.

1423
01:34:22.000 --> 01:34:32.277
But at the same time, I’m very grateful that you all and Sasol have combined in order to preserve some of this history.

1424
01:34:32.277 --> 01:34:40.319
Because I would like for maybe my great-great-grandchildren one day to know that there was a community here.

1425
01:34:40.319 --> 01:34:43.000
We did have cohesiveness, a camaraderie.

1426
01:34:43.000 --> 01:34:47.915
Families got along with each other and whatever else and all.

1427
01:34:47.915 --> 01:34:53.277
I would just like for them to know that we did exist.

1428
01:34:53.277 --> 01:34:53.723
Yeah.

1429
01:34:53.723 --> 01:35:02.213
And when I was telling you that Daniel Braxton was one of the ones that visited [the Paradise Club].

1430
01:35:02.213 --> 01:35:04.000
He was an adult.

1431
01:35:04.000 --> 01:35:04.894
The Paradise.

1432
01:35:04.894 --> 01:35:09.809
And I had an Uncle Robert that went to the Paradise.

1433
01:35:09.809 --> 01:35:14.723
I knew it would come to me in a little while.

1434
01:35:14.723 --> 01:35:21.426
And Uncle Robert and Daniel used to go shoot dice all the time. [laughs] Yeah.

1435
01:35:21.426 --> 01:35:23.660
<v ARSENEAULT>People don’t shoot dice anymore.

1436
01:35:23.660 --> 01:35:25.000
<v PAYNE>No. No.

1437
01:35:25.000 --> 01:35:27.120
<v ARSENEAULT>Well Beyoncé did in one of her videos.

1438
01:35:27.120 --> 01:35:28.976
I guess people do still shoot dice.

1439
01:35:28.976 --> 01:35:29.771
I don't know.

1440
01:35:29.771 --> 01:35:30.831
I don’t see it.

1441
01:35:30.831 --> 01:35:34.012
<v PAYNE>I guess if you go to the casino, they roll them. [laughs]

1442
01:35:34.012 --> 01:35:34.807
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, that’s right.

1443
01:35:34.807 --> 01:35:35.337
<v PAYNE>Yeah, yeah.

1444
01:35:35.337 --> 01:35:35.867
<v ARSENEAULT>All righty.

1445
01:35:35.867 --> 01:35:38.253
Well, thank you so much for talking with us.

1446
01:35:38.253 --> 01:35:40.373
<v PAYNE>I hope I’ve said something that was beneficial.

1447
01:35:40.373 --> 01:35:41.169
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, most definitely.

1448
01:35:41.169 --> 01:35:41.964
<v PAYNE>I hope so.

1449
01:35:41.964 --> 01:35:46.205
<v ARSENEAULT>And I’ll send you the link to this so you can share it with your grandbabies.

1450
01:35:46.205 --> 01:35:47.000
<v PAYNE>All right.

1451
01:35:47.000 --> 01:35:47.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4483.

1452
01:35:47.429 --> 01:35:47.857
End Session I.]
